 failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-y1xEWvQg' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-09-14 02:30:42.055 xcodebuild[77784:3233446]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-09-14 02:30:42.226 xcodebuild[77784:3233443] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\n352979f feat(semantic-vm): Phase 3 Streams, FFI, consumers and handoff demo\\n2bf3977 feat(semantic-vm): Phase 1 Core Linear Interpreter & Pure-data wait sets\\n2b4e081 feat(semantic-vm): Phase 2 AST Lowering Compiler\\n2ce4451 docs: add §7 Universal Continuation Format as proposed feature\\n394940e docs: append Universal Continuation Format to semantic vm design\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md docs/agents/build-n-test.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md docs/agents/build-n-test.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\\n\\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\\n\\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\\n\\n### A. Logic & Correctness\\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\\n\\n### B. Security & Credentials\\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\\n\\n### C. Architecture & API Design\\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\\n\\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\\n\\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\\n\\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\\n2. **Explain the \\\"Why\\\"**: Don't just say \\\"change this\\\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \\\"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\\\").\\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\\n---\\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\\n---\\n\\n# BUILD & TEST\\n\\n## Commands\\n\\n```sh\\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\\nbb test              # Run all tests (JVM, Node/CLJS, Dart/CLJD)\\nbb test:clj          # JVM tests only (or: clj -M:test)\\nbb test:cljs         # ClojureScript / Node tests (via shadow-cljs :cljs alias)\\nbb test:cljd         # ClojureDart tests (requires flutter on PATH)\\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\\n\\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\\n\\n# ClojureDart\\nclj -M:cljd compile\\n\\n# Linting\\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\\n```\\n\\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\\n\\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\\n\\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\\n\\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\\n\\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\\n\\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\\n\\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\\n\\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\\n   ```sh\\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\\n   ```\\n\\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\\n\\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\\n   Common patterns:\\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\\n\\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '446,508p' src/cljc/yin/repl/core.cljc; nl -ba test/yin/repl_core_test.cljc\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '446,508p' src/cljc/yin/repl/core.cljc; nl -ba test/yin/repl_core_test.cljc\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"(defn- eval-ast\\n  \\\"Evaluate a compiled AST.  The ast-walker evaluates it directly; the\\n   semantic VM executes only code segments, so its AST travels the program\\n   medium as datoms and is lowered by the session's loader.\\\"\\n  [state ast]\\n  (cond\\n    (:ingress-loss? state) [state (str \\\"Error: \\\" ingress-loss-text)]\\n    (= :semantic (:vm-type state)) (eval-datoms state (vm/ast->datoms ast))\\n    :else (let [state' (inject-last-value state)]\\n            (finalize-eval state state' (vm/eval (:vm state') ast)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- eval-datoms\\n  \\\"Evaluate one datom-literal program by appending it through the program\\n   medium's writer and then driving observer coordination, which loads and\\n   runs every observed batch.\\n\\n   The observer recovers across a gap on its own, but the shell reads the gap\\n   count around the round: an increase means one or more batches were never\\n   run, so resuming as though execution were complete would report a result\\n   built on programs the VM never saw.  The loss is reported and the shell\\n   refuses further evaluation until `(reset)`.\\n\\n   A completed program's lexical environment does not outlive it: the VM's\\n   environment is restored to the one the round began with, as `vm/eval`\\n   does.  A round that throws is consumed by `consume-failed-round`.\\\"\\n  [state datoms]\\n  (if (:ingress-loss? state)\\n    [state (str \\\"Error: \\\" ingress-loss-text)]\\n    (let [state' (inject-last-value state)\\n          append (stream/append! (:program-stream state') (vec datoms))]\\n      (if-not (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome append))\\n        [state (str \\\"Error: datom batch not ingested: \\\"\\n                    (name (:dao.stream/outcome append)))]\\n        (try\\n          (let [observer0 (:observer state')\\n                gaps-before (:ingress-gaps observer0 0)\\n                {:keys [observer] vm :consumer}\\n                (observer/run-on-stream {:observer observer0,\\n                                         :consumer (:vm state')}\\n                                        engine/ready-for-ingress?\\n                                        (:load-program state')\\n                                        run-vm)\\n                state'' (assoc state' :observer observer :vm vm)]\\n            (cond\\n              (> (:ingress-gaps observer 0) gaps-before)\\n              [(assoc state'' :ingress-loss? true)\\n               (str \\\"Error: \\\" ingress-loss-text)]\\n\\n              (vm/halted? vm)\\n              (finalize-eval state state''\\n                             (engine/restore-initial-env (:env (:vm state')) vm))\\n\\n              :else\\n              (throw\\n                (ex-info\\n                  \\\"Datom stream did not form a complete, runnable Yin VM program.\\nHint: If you wanted to evaluate these datoms as data, use a quote: '[[...]]\\\"\\n                  {:root-id (:root-id (vm/index-datoms datoms))\\n                   :datom-count (count datoms)}))))\\n          (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs js/Error) error\\n            (consume-failed-round state error)))))))\\n\\n     1\\t(ns yin.repl-core-test\\n     2\\t  (:require [clojure.string :as str]\\n     3\\t            [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\\n     4\\t            [dao.stream :as stream]\\n     5\\t            [dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ring]\\n     6\\t            [yin.repl.core :as core]\\n     7\\t            [dao.stream.observer :as observer]))\\n     8\\t\\n     9\\t\\n    10\\t(defn- handle\\n    11\\t  [capacity]\\n    12\\t  (:dao.stream/handle\\n    13\\t    (ring/create! {:dao.stream/type ring/transport-type\\n    14\\t                   ring/capacity-key capacity})))\\n    15\\t\\n    16\\t\\n    17\\t(defn- oldest\\n    18\\t  [h]\\n    19\\t  (:dao.stream/cursor (stream/cursor h :dao.stream/oldest)))\\n    20\\t\\n    21\\t\\n    22\\t(defn- evaluate\\n    23\\t  \\\"Evaluate each line in order against one state value, returning the last\\n    24\\t   result text.  The core is synchronous, so a test needs no driver.\\\"\\n    25\\t  [state lines]\\n    26\\t  (reduce (fn [[state _] line] (core/eval-input state line))\\n    27\\t          [state nil]\\n    28\\t          lines))\\n    29\\t\\n    30\\t\\n    31\\t(deftest fresh-state-is-a-semantic-shell-with-an-untried-ledger\\n    32\\t  (let [state (core/create-state)]\\n    33\\t    (is (= :semantic (:vm-type state))\\n    34\\t        \\\"the semantic VM is the default after Phase 4 (yin.vm.semantic.md §8)\\\")\\n    35\\t    (is (= :clojure (:lang state)))\\n    36\\t    (is (true? (:running? state)))\\n    37\\t    (is (= :untried (get-in state [:ledger :output]))\\n    38\\t        \\\"an idle medium has no last operation, so the ledger says so\\\")\\n    39\\t    (is (some? (:program-stream state))\\n    40\\t        \\\"the shell owns the program medium's writer handle\\\")\\n    41\\t    (is (some? (:observer state))\\n    42\\t        \\\"an observer is attached beside the VM, not inside it\\\")\\n    43\\t    (is (zero? (:ingress-gaps (:observer state))))\\n    44\\t    (is (false? (:ingress-loss? state)))))\\n    45\\t\\n    46\\t\\n    47\\t(deftest ordinary-source-evaluates-locally\\n    48\\t  (let [[_ result] (core/eval-input (core/create-state) \\\"(+ 1 2)\\\")]\\n    49\\t    (is (= \\\"3\\\" result))))\\n    50\\t\\n    51\\t\\n    52\\t(deftest printed-output-precedes-the-value\\n    53\\t  (let [[state result] (core/eval-input (core/create-state) \\\"(println \\\\\\\"hi\\\\\\\")\\\")]\\n    54\\t    (is (= \\\"hi\\\\nnil\\\" result))\\n    55\\t    (is (= :dao.stream/blocked (get-in state [:ledger :output]))\\n    56\\t        \\\"the drain records the outcome that ended it\\\")))\\n    57\\t\\n    58\\t\\n    59\\t(deftest value-history-is-injected-for-the-next-evaluation\\n    60\\t  (let [[_ result] (evaluate (core/create-state) [\\\"(+ 1 2)\\\" \\\"(+ *1 1)\\\"])]\\n    61\\t    (is (= \\\"4\\\" result))))\\n    62\\t\\n    63\\t\\n    64\\t(deftest multi-line-input-accumulates-until-the-brackets-balance\\n    65\\t  (let [[state partial] (core/eval-input (core/create-state) \\\"(+ 1\\\")\\n    66\\t        [state' result] (core/eval-input state \\\" 2)\\\")]\\n    67\\t    (is (= \\\"\\\" partial))\\n    68\\t    (is (= \\\"(+ 1\\\" (:pending-input state)))\\n    69\\t    (is (= \\\"3\\\" result))\\n    70\\t    (is (nil? (:pending-input state')))))\\n    71\\t\\n    72\\t\\n    73\\t(deftest the-vm-command-offers-the-ast-walker-and-the-semantic-vm\\n    74\\t  (let [[state result] (core/eval-input (core/create-state) \\\"(vm :ast-walker)\\\")\\n    75\\t        [state' semantic] (core/eval-input state \\\"(vm :semantic)\\\")\\n    76\\t        [_ rejected] (core/eval-input state' \\\"(vm :register)\\\")]\\n    77\\t    (is (= \\\"Switched to ASTWalkerVM (store cleared)\\\" result))\\n    78\\t    (is (= \\\"Switched to SemanticVM (store cleared)\\\" semantic))\\n    79\\t    (is (= :semantic (:vm-type state')))\\n    80\\t    (is (str/includes? rejected \\\"Unknown Yin REPL VM type\\\"))\\n    81\\t    (is (str/includes? rejected \\\":semantic\\\"))))\\n    82\\t\\n    83\\t\\n    84\\t(deftest the-semantic-vm-evaluates-through-the-lowering-loader\\n    85\\t  ;; The program medium is shared by every state threaded from one session,\\n    86\\t  ;; so each case starts from its own session rather than a stale state.\\n    87\\t  (let [semantic-state #(first (core/eval-input (core/create-state) \\\"(vm :semantic)\\\"))]\\n    88\\t    (testing \\\"source lowers to a code segment and runs\\\"\\n    89\\t      (let [[state' result] (core/eval-input (semantic-state) \\\"(+ 1 2)\\\")]\\n    90\\t        (is (= \\\"3\\\" result))\\n    91\\t        (is (= 1 (count (:code (:vm state')))))))\\n    92\\t    (testing \\\"definitions, recursion, and history span successive programs\\\"\\n    93\\t      (let [[state' result]\\n    94\\t            (evaluate (semantic-state)\\n    95\\t                      [\\\"(defn sum-to [n] (if (= n 0) 0 (+ n (sum-to (- n 1)))))\\\"\\n    96\\t                       \\\"(sum-to 100)\\\"\\n    97\\t                       \\\"(+ *1 1)\\\"])]\\n    98\\t        (is (= \\\"5051\\\" result))\\n    99\\t        (is (= 3 (count (:code (:vm state'))))\\n   100\\t            \\\"each program is its own segment; earlier closures still resolve\\\")))\\n   101\\t    (testing \\\"printed output precedes the value\\\"\\n   102\\t      (is (= \\\"hi\\\\nnil\\\" (second (core/eval-input (semantic-state) \\\"(println \\\\\\\"hi\\\\\\\")\\\")))))\\n   103\\t    (testing \\\"a datom literal is lowered like compiled source\\\"\\n   104\\t      (is (= \\\"99\\\" (second (core/eval-input (semantic-state)\\n   105\\t                                           \\\"[[-1 :yin/type :literal 0 1]\\n   106\\t                                             [-1 :yin/value 99 0 1]]\\\")))))\\n   107\\t    (testing \\\"reset keeps the evaluator and rebuilds its session\\\"\\n   108\\t      (let [[state' message] (core/eval-input (semantic-state) \\\"(reset)\\\")]\\n   109\\t        (is (= \\\"SemanticVM reset\\\" message))\\n   110\\t        (is (= :semantic (get-in (core/repl-state state') [:vm :type])))\\n   111\\t        (is (= \\\"7\\\" (second (core/eval-input state' \\\"(+ 3 4)\\\"))))))))\\n   112\\t\\n   113\\t\\n   114\\t(deftest a-failed-input-is-consumed-exactly-once\\n   115\\t  (doseq [vm-type [:semantic :ast-walker]]\\n   116\\t    (testing (str vm-type \\\" : an evaluation error and its effects do not replay\\\")\\n   117\\t      (let [state (first (core/eval-input (core/create-state) (str \\\"(vm \\\" vm-type \\\")\\\")))\\n   118\\t            [state' failed] (core/eval-input state \\\"(do (println \\\\\\\"before\\\\\\\") (/ 1 0))\\\")\\n   119\\t            [state'' next-result] (core/eval-input state' \\\"(+ 1 2)\\\")]\\n   120\\t        (is (str/starts-with? failed \\\"before\\\\n\\\"))\\n   121\\t        (is (str/includes? failed \\\"Error: \\\"))\\n   122\\t        (is (= \\\"3\\\" next-result) \\\"the next input runs alone\\\")\\n   123\\t        (is (= \\\"4\\\" (second (core/eval-input state'' \\\"(+ *1 1)\\\")))\\n   124\\t            \\\"observation progress and history continue past the failure\\\"))))\\n   125\\t  (testing \\\"a batch the loader rejects is consumed, not re-read\\\"\\n   126\\t    (let [[state failed] (core/eval-input (core/create-state)\\n   127\\t                                          \\\"[[1 :a 1 0 true] [1 :b 2 0 true]]\\\")]\\n   128\\t      (is (str/starts-with? failed \\\"Error: \\\"))\\n   129\\t      (is (= \\\"3\\\" (second (core/eval-input state \\\"(+ 1 2)\\\")))))))\\n   130\\t\\n   131\\t\\n   132\\t(deftest lexical-scope-does-not-escape-a-top-level-evaluation\\n   133\\t  (doseq [vm-type [:semantic :ast-walker]]\\n   134\\t    (testing (str vm-type)\\n   135\\t      (let [state (first (core/eval-input (core/create-state) (str \\\"(vm \\\" vm-type \\\")\\\")))\\n   136\\t            [state' _] (evaluate state [\\\"(def x 1)\\\" \\\"(let [x 7] x)\\\"])\\n   137\\t            [_ result] (core/eval-input state' \\\"x\\\")]\\n   138\\t        (is (= \\\"1\\\" result))))))\\n   139\\t\\n   140\\t\\n   141\\t(deftest reset-and-vm-selection-clear-the-value-history\\n   142\\t  (doseq [command [\\\"(reset)\\\" \\\"(vm :semantic)\\\"]]\\n   143\\t    (testing command\\n   144\\t      (let [[state _] (evaluate (core/create-state) [\\\"(fn [x] (+ x 1))\\\" command])\\n   145\\t            [state' result] (core/eval-input state \\\"(*1 4)\\\")]\\n   146\\t        (is (nil? (:last-value state)) \\\"no closure outlives its code segment\\\")\\n   147\\t        (is (str/starts-with? result \\\"Error: \\\"))\\n   148\\t        (is (true? (:running? state')))))))\\n   149\\t\\n   150\\t\\n   151\\t(deftest language-and-compile-commands-behave-as-they-do-in-v1\\n   152\\t  (let [[state result] (core/eval-input (core/create-state) \\\"(lang :python)\\\")\\n   153\\t        [_ compiled] (core/eval-input state \\\"(compile \\\\\\\"1 + 2\\\\\\\")\\\")]\\n   154\\t    (is (= \\\"Switched to Python\\\" result))\\n   155\\t    (is (str/includes? compiled \\\"AST:\\\"))\\n   156\\t    (is (str/includes? compiled \\\"Datoms:\\\"))))\\n   157\\t\\n   158\\t\\n   159\\t(deftest reset-rebuilds-the-vm-and-its-attachment\\n   160\\t  (let [[state _] (evaluate (core/create-state) [\\\"(+ 1 2)\\\"])\\n   161\\t        [state' message] (core/eval-input state \\\"(reset)\\\")]\\n   162\\t    (is (= \\\"SemanticVM reset\\\" message))\\n   163\\t    (is (not (identical? (:vm state) (:vm state'))))\\n   164\\t    (is (not (identical? (:program-stream state) (:program-stream state')))\\n   165\\t        \\\"the program medium is rebuilt with the VM\\\")\\n   166\\t    (is (not (identical? (:observer state) (:observer state')))\\n   167\\t        \\\"the observer is reattached to the new medium\\\")\\n   168\\t    (is (zero? (:ingress-gaps (:observer state'))))\\n   169\\t    (is (identical? (:output-stream state) (:output-stream state'))\\n   170\\t        \\\"the output medium belongs to the composition, not to the VM\\\")))\\n   171\\t\\n   172\\t\\n   173\\t(deftest the-program-medium-attaches-by-descriptor\\n   174\\t  (let [state (core/create-state)\\n   175\\t        writer (:program-stream state)\\n   176\\t        descriptor (:dao.stream/descriptor (stream/descriptor writer))\\n   177\\t        attach! (ring/make-attacher {(:dao.stream/identity descriptor) writer})\\n   178\\t        observer (observer/attach attach! descriptor)]\\n   179\\t    (stream/append! writer [[:batch]])\\n   180\\t    (let [observed (observer/observe-next observer)]\\n   181\\t      (is (= :ok (:status observed)))\\n   182\\t      (is (= [[:batch]] (:batch observed))\\n   183\\t          \\\"the resolver maps the descriptor's identity to the owner handle\\\"))))\\n   184\\t\\n   185\\t\\n   186\\t(deftest quit-stops-the-shell-without-touching-the-host\\n   187\\t  (let [[state result] (core/eval-input (core/create-state) \\\"(quit)\\\")]\\n   188\\t    (is (= \\\"Bye\\\" result))\\n   189\\t    (is (false? (:running? state)))))\\n   190\\t\\n   191\\t\\n   192\\t(deftest telemetry-is-rejected-and-names-the-v1-repl\\n   193\\t  (let [[state result] (core/eval-input (core/create-state) \\\"(telemetry)\\\")]\\n   194\\t    (is (str/includes? result \\\"yin.repl\\\"))\\n   195\\t    (is (true? (:running? state)))))\\n   196\\t\\n   197\\t\\n   198\\t(deftest repl-state-reports-the-ledger-rather-than-asking-a-stream\\n   199\\t  (let [state (core/create-state)\\n   200\\t        summary (core/repl-state state)]\\n   201\\t    (is (= :semantic (get-in summary [:vm :type])))\\n   202\\t    (is (false? (get-in summary [:telemetry :supported?])))\\n   203\\t    (is (= :untried (get-in summary [:output :last-outcome])))\\n   204\\t    (is (contains? (:output summary) :cursor))\\n   205\\t    (let [[_ rendered] (core/eval-input state \\\"(repl-state)\\\")]\\n   206\\t      (is (str/includes? rendered \\\":semantic\\\")))))\\n   207\\t\\n   208\\t\\n   209\\t(deftest datom-literal-evaluation-runs-through-the-program-medium\\n   210\\t  (testing \\\"a runnable datom program evaluates to its value, as in v1\\\"\\n   211\\t    (let [[state result] (core/eval-input (core/create-state)\\n   212\\t                                          \\\"[[-1 :yin/type :literal 0 1]\\n   213\\t                                            [-1 :yin/value 99 0 1]]\\\")]\\n   214\\t      (is (= \\\"99\\\" result))\\n   215\\t      (is (= 99 (:last-value state)))))\\n   216\\t  (testing \\\"a non-program datom stream is reported, never thrown at the host\\\"\\n   217\\t    (let [[state result] (core/eval-input (core/create-state)\\n   218\\t                                          \\\"[[1 :a 1 0 true] [1 :b 2 0 true]]\\\")]\\n   219\\t      (is (str/starts-with? result \\\"Error: \\\"))\\n   220\\t      (is (true? (:running? state))))))\\n   221\\t\\n   222\\t\\n   223\\t(deftest an-ingress-gap-is-fatal-to-evaluation-until-reset\\n   224\\t  (let [state (core/create-state)\\n   225\\t        writer (:program-stream state)]\\n   226\\t    ;; Evict one batch the observer never sees: the shell is the only\\n   227\\t    ;; appender, so only a flood beyond the declared capacity can produce the\\n   228\\t    ;; gap.\\n   229\\t    (doseq [_ (range (inc core/ingress-capacity))]\\n   230\\t      (stream/append! writer [[-1 :yin/type :literal 0 1] [-1 :yin/value 1 0 1]]))\\n   231\\t    (let [[state' result] (core/eval-input state\\n   232\\t                                           \\\"[[-1 :yin/type :literal 0 1]\\n   233\\t                                             [-1 :yin/value 2 0 1]]\\\")]\\n   234\\t      (is (str/starts-with? result \\\"Error: \\\") \\\"the loss is reported\\\")\\n   235\\t      (is (str/includes? result \\\"(reset)\\\"))\\n   236\\t      (is (true? (:ingress-loss? state')))\\n   237\\t      (testing \\\"further evaluation is refused rather than resumed as if complete\\\"\\n   238\\t        (let [[_ refused] (core/eval-input state' \\\"(+ 1 2)\\\")]\\n   239\\t          (is (str/starts-with? refused \\\"Error: \\\"))))\\n   240\\t      (testing \\\"commands still answer, and (reset) recovers the shell\\\"\\n   241\\t        (let [[state'' message] (core/eval-input state' \\\"(reset)\\\")]\\n   242\\t          (is (= \\\"SemanticVM reset\\\" message))\\n   243\\t          (is (false? (:ingress-loss? state'')))\\n   244\\t          (is (zero? (get-in (core/repl-state state'') [:vm :in-stream :gaps])))\\n   245\\t          (let [[_ recovered] (core/eval-input state'' \\\"(+ 1 2)\\\")]\\n   246\\t            (is (= \\\"3\\\" recovered)))\\n   247\\t          (let [[_ datoms] (core/eval-input state''\\n   248\\t                                            \\\"[[-1 :yin/type :literal 0 1]\\n   249\\t                                              [-1 :yin/value 8 0 1]]\\\")]\\n   250\\t            (is (= \\\"8\\\" datoms)\\n   251\\t                \\\"datom evaluation succeeds on the rebuilt attachment\\\")))))))\\n   252\\t\\n   253\\t\\n   254\\t(deftest unknown-commands-and-reader-failures-are-reported-not-thrown\\n   255\\t  (testing \\\"an unknown command\\\"\\n   256\\t    (let [[state result] (core/eval-input (core/create-state) \\\"(nope)\\\")]\\n   257\\t      (is (str/starts-with? result \\\"Error: \\\"))\\n   258\\t      (is (true? (:running? state)))))\\n   259\\t  (testing \\\"unreadable input\\\"\\n   260\\t    (let [[_ result] (core/eval-input (core/create-state) \\\"\\\\\\\"unterminated\\\")]\\n   261\\t      (is (str/starts-with? result \\\"Error: \\\")))))\\n   262\\t\\n   263\\t\\n   264\\t(deftest the-output-drain-is-total-over-next\\n   265\\t  (testing \\\"a gap prints a loss notice and resumes at the recovery cursor\\\"\\n   266\\t    (let [output (handle 2)\\n   267\\t          state (core/create-state {:output-stream output})]\\n   268\\t      (doseq [n (range 5)]\\n   269\\t        (stream/append! output {:type :repl/output :op :print :text (str n)}))\\n   270\\t      (let [[state' text] (core/drain-output state)]\\n   271\\t        (is (str/includes? text \\\"output lost\\\"))\\n   272\\t        (is (str/includes? text \\\"34\\\"))\\n   273\\t        (is (= :dao.stream/blocked (get-in state' [:ledger :output])))\\n   274\\t        (let [[_ text'] (core/drain-output state')]\\n   275\\t          (is (= \\\"\\\" text') \\\"the recovery cursor is retained, not re-read\\\")))))\\n   276\\t  (testing \\\"a closed medium ends the drain and is recorded once\\\"\\n   277\\t    (let [output (handle 8)\\n   278\\t          state (core/create-state {:output-stream output})]\\n   279\\t      (stream/append! output {:type :repl/output :op :print :text \\\"x\\\"})\\n   280\\t      (stream/close! output)\\n   281\\t      (let [[state' text] (core/drain-output state)]\\n   282\\t        (is (str/includes? text \\\"x\\\"))\\n   283\\t        (is (= :dao.stream/end (get-in state' [:ledger :output])))))))\\n   284\\t\\n   285\\t\\n   286\\t(deftest the-output-cursor-is-minted-not-fabricated\\n   287\\t  (let [output (handle 8)\\n   288\\t        state (core/create-state {:output-stream output})]\\n   289\\t    (is (= (oldest output) (:output-cursor state)))))\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"     1\\t# yin.vm.semantic on dao.stream — linear executable datoms\\n     2\\t\\n     3\\tStatus: Phase 0 contract. Sections §1–§6 are promoted verbatim from\\n     4\\t`collab/1789221648668-architect-semantic-vm-v2-design.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`.\\n     5\\tThe opcode table (§2.4) and the attribute tables (§2.2, §2.3) are the\\n     6\\tcontract that `yin.vm/code-schema`, `yin.vm/opcode-table`, and\\n     7\\t`yin.vm.code/well-formed?` implement. The integration and roadmap\\n     8\\tsections remain in the findings note; §8 records the Phase 4 measurements.\\n     9\\t\\n    10\\t---\\n    11\\t\\n    12\\t## §1. Foundational axioms and invariant compliance\\n    13\\t\\n    14\\t### 1.1 The four axioms\\n    15\\t\\n    16\\t**Everything is a Stream.** Code arrives on a program stream as a segment\\n    17\\tvalue; execution is consumption of that stream by an observer, and then\\n    18\\tconsumption of the segment by the machine. Effects the program performs\\n    19\\t(`:stream-put`, `:stream-next`, FFI) are emissions on streams the composition\\n    20\\tsupplied, exactly as for the walker. Optional execution traces are emissions\\n    21\\ttoo (§3.6). What is *not* a stream is the instruction fetch inside a loaded\\n    22\\tsegment, and this is deliberate: a boundary belongs \\\"wherever you need\\n    23\\tdecoupling — and nowhere else\\\" (`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §1). Fetch has\\n    24\\tone consumer, one order, no replay need, and no host boundary; putting a\\n    25\\tstream there is the per-value tax §4 of that note names as hopeless.\\n    26\\t\\n    27\\t**Interpretation Creates Semantics.** `[e :yin.code/op :call]` means nothing\\n    28\\tuntil `yin.vm.semantic` reads it. The same datoms can be read by a query\\n    29\\t(\\\"all call sites of this closure\\\"), a renderer (back to source through\\n    30\\t`:yin.code/source`), a verifier (every block ends in a terminator), or a\\n    31\\tdifferent VM. The mapping from the keyword mnemonic in the datom to the\\n    32\\tinteger opcode in the image is the loader's interpretation, not a fact in\\n    33\\tthe datom. `:yin.code/tail?` is a property of a call in the datoms; only the\\n    34\\timage decides it is a distinct `tailcall` dispatch.\\n    35\\t\\n    36\\t**Code and State are Datoms.** Instructions are entities with attributes.\\n    37\\tExecution state is plain data: the control is `{:segment id :pc n}`, the\\n    38\\tenvironment a map, the operand stack a vector, the continuation a vector of\\n    39\\tframes each `{:pc :env :stack-base :segment}`. The optional trace vocabulary\\n    40\\t(§3.6) deposits exactly these as `[e a v t m]` with `t` the step counter,\\n    41\\tthe \\\"endgame\\\" of `cesk-space-optimization.md` §6: execute fast, emit the\\n    42\\tmachine image as datoms rather than interpret from them.\\n    43\\t\\n    44\\t**Everything is a Continuation.** Park writes `{segment pc env stack k}` into\\n    45\\t`:parked`; resume reads it back. Nothing in that map is a host object except\\n    46\\tvalues the program itself put in `env` or on the stack (host functions\\n    47\\tresolved from primitives, stream handles from the store), which is the same\\n    48\\tserialization limit the walker has today. A continuation shipped through a\\n    49\\tstream names its segment by id; the receiving host resolves the id against\\n    50\\tsegment datoms it holds or was sent alongside.\\n    51\\t\\n    52\\t### 1.2 The six invariants\\n    53\\t\\n    54\\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n    55\\t| Invariant                | How the design honors it                                                         |\\n    56\\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n    57\\t| No hidden global state   | Opcode table is a value in `yin.vm`. Segments live in the VM record under     |\\n    58\\t|                          | `:code`. Primitives, modules, `:make-stream`, bridge handlers are all supplied   |\\n    59\\t|                          | at construction, as for the walker. No `defonce`, no registry.                   |\\n    60\\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n    61\\t| No implicit control flow | Every transfer of control is an instruction datom: `:jump`, `:branch-false`,     |\\n    62\\t|                          | `:call`, `:return`, `:halt`. Sequencing is the explicit `:yin.code/pc` order. No |\\n    63\\t|                          | fallthrough that is not a `pc + 1` visible in the datoms.                        |\\n    64\\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n    65\\t| No callbacks             | Host effects go through `engine/handle-effect` and the FFI request/response      |\\n    66\\t|                          | pair; a blocked read parks in the polling wait set. The VM invokes nothing and   |\\n    67\\t|                          | is invoked by nothing.                                                           |\\n    68\\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n    69\\t| No shared mutable state  | The image array is built once per segment and never written after load. The hot  |\\n    70\\t|                          | loop keeps registers in `loop` locals; the persistent VM record is the only      |\\n    71\\t|                          | state that escapes a step. No mutable deftype fields (a cljd trap on record).    |\\n    72\\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n    73\\t| No layer collapsing      | Lowering (AST datoms → code datoms), loading (code datoms → image), and          |\\n    74\\t|                          | execution (image → transitions) are three functions in two namespaces with data  |\\n    75\\t|                          | between each. The walker keeps interpreting the AST; nothing in the walker       |\\n    76\\t|                          | changes.                                                                         |\\n    77\\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n    78\\t| No assumed graphs        | Branch targets and closure bodies are refs; the loader constructs the pc index   |\\n    79\\t|                          | from tuples in an explicit pass and resolves refs against it. A dangling ref is  |\\n    80\\t|                          | a load error naming the entity, never a runtime lookup.                          |\\n    81\\t+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n    82\\t\\n    83\\t---\\n    84\\t\\n    85\\t## §2. The linear executable datom specification\\n    86\\t\\n    87\\t### 2.1 Tuple format\\n    88\\t\\n    89\\tExecutable datoms are canonical 5-tuples `[e a v t m]`, produced the same\\n    90\\tway `yin.vm/ast->datoms` produces AST datoms: negative tempids for `e`,\\n    91\\t`t` = 0 unless the linearizer is given one, `m` = `dao.datom/default-op`.\\n    92\\tAttributes live under the `:yin.code/*` namespace so that\\n    93\\t`engine/executable-program-datom?` (which selects `:yin/*`) does not confuse\\n    94\\ta code segment with an AST program, and a query can select either view.\\n    95\\t\\n    96\\t### 2.2 The segment\\n    97\\t\\n    98\\tA segment is one entity plus its instructions. It is the unit a program\\n    99\\tstream carries (one batch value), the unit the loader accepts, and the unit\\n   100\\ta closure or continuation names.\\n   101\\t\\n   102\\t+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   103\\t| Attribute                | Value              | Meaning                                                                          |\\n   104\\t+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   105\\t| `:yin.code/type`         | `:segment`         | Marks the segment entity.                                                        |\\n   106\\t+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   107\\t| `:yin.code/length`       | int                | Number of instructions; pcs are `0 .. length-1`, dense.                          |\\n   108\\t+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   109\\t| `:yin.code/derived-from` | ref (AST root eid) | Provenance; optional when hand-assembled.                                        |\\n   110\\t+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   111\\t| `:yin.code/hash`         | string             | Reserved: content address of the canonical instruction datoms, for dedup and     |\\n   112\\t|                          |                    | verification (`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.3). Not required in Phase 1.      |\\n   113\\t+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   114\\t\\n   115\\tEntry is pc 0 by rule. Making entry a fact would let a segment disagree with\\n   116\\titself; a rule cannot.\\n   117\\t\\n   118\\t### 2.3 The instruction\\n   119\\t\\n   120\\tEvery instruction entity carries three structural facts and one or more\\n   121\\toperand facts.\\n   122\\t\\n   123\\t+---------------------+--------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+\\n   124\\t| Attribute           | Value                    | Required on                                           |\\n   125\\t+---------------------+--------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+\\n   126\\t| `:yin.code/segment` | ref (segment eid)        | every instruction                                     |\\n   127\\t+---------------------+--------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+\\n   128\\t| `:yin.code/pc`      | int                      | every instruction                                     |\\n   129\\t+---------------------+--------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+\\n   130\\t| `:yin.code/op`      | keyword mnemonic (below) | every instruction                                     |\\n   131\\t+---------------------+--------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+\\n   132\\t| `:yin.code/source`  | ref (AST node eid)       | every lowered instruction; absent when hand-assembled |\\n   133\\t+---------------------+--------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+\\n   134\\t\\n   135\\tLinearity is therefore an **explicit fact**, `:yin.code/pc`, not the vector\\n   136\\torder of the batch and not entity-id arithmetic. Entity ids are tempids that\\n   137\\ta transactor may remap; vector order is a transport accident. The\\n   138\\twell-formedness rule (§2.6) requires the batch to be *sorted* by pc so a\\n   139\\tloader can stream it, but the pc fact is the truth.\\n   140\\t\\n   141\\tBranch and closure targets are **refs to instruction entities**\\n   142\\t(`:yin.code/target`, `:yin.code/body`), not pc integers. The loader resolves\\n   143\\teach ref to a pc through the index it builds. This keeps the datom semantic\\n   144\\t(\\\"this branch goes to that instruction\\\"), survives id remapping when both\\n   145\\tends are remapped together, and is checkable.\\n   146\\t\\n   147\\t### 2.4 The core semantic primitives\\n   148\\t\\n   149\\tMachine model: an **accumulator machine with an operand stack** (§4). `val`\\n   150\\tis the accumulator; `St` the operand stack; `K` the continuation of return\\n   151\\tframes. This is the stack-class formulation `streams-all-the-way-down.md` §3\\n   152\\tsays survives the log-structured reading; a register model would not.\\n   153\\t\\n   154\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   155\\t| `:yin.code/op`          | Operand attributes                            | Effect on the machine                                                            |\\n   156\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   157\\t| `:const`                | `:yin.code/value v`                           | `val ← v`                                                                        |\\n   158\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   159\\t| `:var`                  | `:yin.code/name sym`                          | `val ← resolve(E, S, prims, modules, sym)`                                       |\\n   160\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   161\\t| `:closure`              | `:yin.code/params [..]`, `:yin.code/body ref` | `val ← {:type :closure :params .. :entry pc(body) :segment seg :env E}`          |\\n   162\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   163\\t| `:push`                 | —                                             | `St ← St ⧺ [val]`                                                                |\\n   164\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   165\\t| `:call`                 | `:yin.code/argc n`, `:yin.code/tail? bool`    | pop `n` args and the operator below them; apply (§4.3)                           |\\n   166\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   167\\t| `:return`               | —                                             | pop frame from `K`; restore `pc`, `E`, stack base; `val` unchanged; empty `K`    |\\n   168\\t|                         |                                               | halts                                                                            |\\n   169\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   170\\t| `:jump`                 | `:yin.code/target ref`                        | `pc ← target`                                                                    |\\n   171\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   172\\t| `:branch-false`         | `:yin.code/target ref`                        | `pc ← val ? pc+1 : target`                                                       |\\n   173\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   174\\t| `:halt`                 | —                                             | halt with `val` as the result                                                    |\\n   175\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   176\\t| `:gensym`               | `:yin.code/prefix`                            | `val ← fresh id`; id counter advances                                            |\\n   177\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   178\\t| `:store-get`            | `:yin.code/key`                               | `val ← S[key]`                                                                   |\\n   179\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   180\\t| `:store-put`            | `:yin.code/key`, `:yin.code/value`            | `S[key] ← v; val ← v`                                                            |\\n   181\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   182\\t| `:stream-make`          | `:yin.code/buffer`                            | effect `:stream/make` via engine                                                 |\\n   183\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   184\\t| `:stream-put`           | —                                             | target ref popped from `St`, value in `val`; effect `:stream/put`                |\\n   185\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   186\\t| `:stream-cursor`        | —                                             | source ref in `val`; effect `:stream/cursor`                                     |\\n   187\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   188\\t| `:stream-next`          | —                                             | cursor ref in `val`; effect `:stream/next`                                       |\\n   189\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   190\\t| `:stream-close`         | —                                             | source ref in `val`; effect `:stream/close`                                      |\\n   191\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   192\\t| `:park`                 | —                                             | `engine/park-continuation` with the current frame                                |\\n   193\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   194\\t| `:resume`               | `:yin.code/parked-id`                         | resume value in `val`; `engine/resume-continuation`                              |\\n   195\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   196\\t| `:current-continuation` | —                                             | `val ← {:type :reified-continuation :segment :pc :env :stack :k}`                |\\n   197\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   198\\t| `:ffi-call`             | `:yin.code/ffi-op kw`, `:yin.code/argc n`     | pop `n` args; park-and-call over the FFI pair                                    |\\n   199\\t+-------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   200\\t\\n   201\\tThis is the AST vocabulary of `yin.vm/ast->datoms` made linear, one for\\n   202\\tone, plus the three sequencing primitives (`:push`, `:jump`, `:branch-false`)\\n   203\\tand `:halt`. `:yin/macro-expand` and `:vm/store-update` are outside both\\n   204\\tevaluators' supported corpus and are rejected at lowering with an error\\n   205\\tnaming the node.\\n   206\\t\\n   207\\t**Opcode integers.** `yin.vm/opcode-table` already assigns integers to\\n   208\\t`:literal :load-var :lambda :call :return :branch :jump :gensym :store-get\\n   209\\t:store-put :stream-make :stream-put :stream-cursor :stream-next :stream-close\\n   210\\t:park :resume :current-cont :tailcall :dao.stream.apply/call`. The loader\\n   211\\tmaps mnemonics onto that table (`:const`→`:literal`, `:var`→`:load-var`,\\n   212\\t`:closure`→`:lambda`, `:branch-false`→`:branch`, `:ffi-call`→\\n   213\\t`:dao.stream.apply/call`, `:call`+`:tail? true`→`:tailcall`) and the table\\n   214\\tgains `:push 22` and `:halt 23`. `:move` stays unused. Keeping the datom\\n   215\\tmnemonics semantic and the image integers mechanical is the point: a query\\n   216\\tasks for `:call`, the dispatch switches on `5` or `20`.\\n   217\\t\\n   218\\t### 2.5 Constants and literals\\n   219\\t\\n   220\\tA literal lives inline in the `v` slot of `:yin.code/value`, polymorphic\\n   221\\texactly as `:yin/value` is today (numbers, strings, keywords, vectors, maps,\\n   222\\tsets, nil). Host functions never appear in code datoms; a primitive is named\\n   223\\tby symbol in `:var` and resolved at run time through the supplied\\n   224\\t`:primitives`, which is how the walker already avoids putting host objects in\\n   225\\tthe program. A shared or large constant pool (`:yin.code/const-ref` to a\\n   226\\tconstant entity) is reserved and not needed for the current corpus.\\n   227\\t\\n   228\\t### 2.6 Well-formedness (checked by the loader, tested in Phase 0)\\n   229\\t\\n   230\\t1. Exactly one entity with `:yin.code/type :segment` per batch.\\n   231\\t2. Instruction pcs are exactly `0 .. length-1`, each once.\\n   232\\t3. The batch is sorted by pc (so a loader can fill the array in one pass).\\n   233\\t4. Every `:yin.code/target` and `:yin.code/body` resolves to an instruction\\n   234\\t   of the same segment.\\n   235\\t5. Every basic block ends in a terminator (`:jump`, `:return`, `:halt`) or\\n   236\\t   falls into a labelled successor; pc `length-1` is a terminator.\\n   237\\t6. Every `:call`/`:ffi-call` has a non-negative `:yin.code/argc`.\\n   238\\t\\n   239\\tViolation is a load error naming the entity and rule. The loader is total\\n   240\\tover the outcomes of its inputs; it does not guess.\\n   241\\t\\n   242\\t### 2.7 A worked segment\\n   243\\t\\n   244\\t`((fn [x] (+ x 1)) 10)` lowers to (pcs shown, refs written as `→pc`):\\n   245\\t\\n   246\\t```\\n   247\\tseg   :yin.code/type :segment   :yin.code/length 14\\n   248\\t0  :closure   params [x]  body →6\\n   249\\t1  :push\\n   250\\t2  :const     10\\n   251\\t3  :push\\n   252\\t4  :call      argc 1  tail? false\\n   253\\t5  :halt\\n   254\\t6  :var       +               ; body of the lambda, out of line\\n   255\\t7  :push\\n   256\\t8  :var       x\\n   257\\t9  :push\\n   258\\t10 :const     1\\n   259\\t11 :push\\n   260\\t12 :call      argc 2  tail? true\\n   261\\t13 :return\\n   262\\t```\\n   263\\t\\n   264\\tThe main sequence (pcs 0–5) ends in `halt`; the body (pcs 6–13) ends in\\n   265\\t`return`. In datoms, pc 4 is:\\n   266\\t\\n   267\\t```\\n   268\\t[-20 :yin.code/segment -1  0 :db/add]\\n   269\\t[-20 :yin.code/pc       4  0 :db/add]\\n   270\\t[-20 :yin.code/op   :call  0 :db/add]\\n   271\\t[-20 :yin.code/argc     1  0 :db/add]\\n   272\\t[-20 :yin.code/tail? false 0 :db/add]\\n   273\\t[-20 :yin.code/source  -16 0 :db/add]   ; the :application AST node\\n   274\\t```\\n   275\\t\\n   276\\tSix datoms for a call site; five for most instructions. Load cost is linear\\n   277\\tin datoms once per segment. Execution cost is per instruction, never per\\n   278\\tdatom.\\n   279\\t\\n   280\\t---\\n   281\\t\\n   282\\t## §3. The semantic VM as a DaoStream v2 interpreter\\n   283\\t\\n   284\\t### 3.1 Consumption: observer, loader, image\\n   285\\t\\n   286\\tThe VM implements `IVM`; program ingress is exactly the walker's shape:\\n   287\\t\\n   288\\t- The host composes a program medium, attaches `yin.vm.stream-observer`\\n   289\\t  to it, and drives `observer/run-on-stream` with `engine/ready-for-ingress?`,\\n   290\\t  a loader, and `vm/run`. The observer owns the handle, the cursor, and gap\\n   291\\t  accounting. The VM holds no program stream.\\n   292\\t- The loader for this evaluator is `semantic/vm-load-program`: it accepts a\\n   293\\t  batch of `:yin.code/*` datoms, validates §2.6, builds the image, stores\\n   294\\t  it under `:code {segment-id image}`, and sets control to\\n   295\\t  `{:segment id :pc 0}`. Segment identity is stable: an id already holding\\n   296\\t  a different image is a load error, and an identical reload is accepted —\\n   297\\t  continuations name segments by id, so an id must never come to mean\\n   298\\t  different code. A composition that carries `:yin/*` AST datoms on\\n   299\\t  its program stream hands the observer `(comp semantic/vm-load-program\\n   300\\t  linearize/lower)` instead; the loader is composition-supplied, so no\\n   301\\t  evaluator learns which form travels. `yin.repl.core/make-session` is\\n   302\\t  where that choice is made.\\n   303\\t\\n   304\\tThe step loop is then: `(aget code pc)` → integer `case` → transition. No\\n   305\\thandle, no cursor, no `next` inside a segment.\\n   306\\t\\n   307\\t### 3.2 Why a branch is not a cursor move\\n   308\\t\\n   309\\tThe contract's cursor is \\\"a position, not a claim\\\" and comes only from the\\n   310\\tstream: minted at `:oldest`/`:newest`, returned as a successor, or recovered\\n   311\\tfrom a `gap`. There is no `seek`, and reaching for a transport-owned position\\n   312\\tanchor is forbidden for contract-generic code. Three designs were weighed:\\n   313\\t\\n   314\\t+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   315\\t| Design                                                                         | Contract status                                                             | Cost                                                                             | Verdict                                                                          |\\n   316\\t+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   317\\t| Instruction stream, `next` per fetch, branch by re-minting a block's `:oldest` | legal only if every basic block is its own stream, delivered as descriptors | one stream operation per branch, one attach per closure body, per-value boundary | rejected: it is the per-scalar boundary §4 of the streams note names as          |\\n   318\\t|                                                                                |                                                                             | tax on every instruction                                                         | hopeless, and a loop body would re-mint on every iteration                       |\\n   319\\t+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   320\\t| Transport-owned position anchors as jump targets                               | illegal for contract-generic code (Surfaces)                                | —                                                                                | rejected                                                                         |\\n   321\\t+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   322\\t| Segment as batch value; pc indexes the loaded image                            | legal; the observer already delivers batches                                | one `next` per segment                                                           | **adopted**                                                                      |\\n   323\\t+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   324\\t\\n   325\\tThe cursor-as-program-counter thesis of the streams note remains true where\\n   326\\tit applies: an execution *trace* emitted to a stream is a straight line, and\\n   327\\ta debugger's cursor over it is a pc in time (§3.6).\\n   328\\t\\n   329\\t### 3.3 Read outcomes at the two boundaries\\n   330\\t\\n   331\\t*Program boundary* (observer, generic): `ok` loads and runs; `blocked` ends\\n   332\\tthe round with the cursor retained; `end` ends the session; `gap` commits\\n   333\\tthe recovery cursor and counts the loss (an evicted segment is a program\\n   334\\tthat never ran; the REPL latch stays host policy); `cursor-mismatch`,\\n   335\\t`invalid-cursor`, `transport-error` throw naming the outcome. Unchanged from\\n   336\\tthe divergence register.\\n   337\\t\\n   338\\t*Data boundary* (instructions, via `engine/handle-effect`): identical to the\\n   339\\twalker because the handlers are shared.\\n   340\\t\\n   341\\t+------------------+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------+-------------+------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------+\\n   342\\t| Instruction      | `ok`                                 | `blocked`/`full`              | `end`       | `gap`                                                | terminal                                                  |\\n   343\\t+------------------+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------+-------------+------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------+\\n   344\\t| `:stream-next`   | `val ← value`, store cursor advanced | park with a wait entry (§3.5) | `val ← nil` | `val ← :dao.stream/gap`, cursor advanced to recovery | error naming outcome                                      |\\n   345\\t+------------------+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------+-------------+------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------+\\n   346\\t| `:stream-put`    | `val ← value`                        | park (`full`)                 | —           | —                                                    | `closed`, `invalid-value`, `transport-error` are errors   |\\n   347\\t+------------------+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------+-------------+------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------+\\n   348\\t| `:stream-cursor` | `val ← cursor-ref`                   | —                             | —           | —                                                    | `invalid-anchor`, `closed`, `transport-error` are errors  |\\n   349\\t+------------------+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------+-------------+------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------+\\n   350\\t| `:stream-make`   | `val ← stream-ref`                   | —                             | —           | —                                                    | `invalid-spec`, `not-found`, `transport-error` are errors |\\n   351\\t+------------------+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------+-------------+------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------+\\n   352\\t| `:stream-close`  | `val ← nil`                          | —                             | —           | —                                                    | `{ok}` only                                               |\\n   353\\t+------------------+--------------------------------------+-------------------------------+-------------+------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------+\\n   354\\t\\n   355\\tUnder a ring-buffer composition `full` never occurs and loss surfaces as a\\n   356\\treader `gap`; the retention divergence stands as written.\\n   357\\t\\n   358\\t### 3.4 FFI through `dao.stream.apply`\\n   359\\t\\n   360\\t`:ffi-call` pops `argc` arguments and runs the semantic `park-and-call`:\\n   361\\tcheck the pair (`ffi/require-call-pair!`) **before** parking; park the frame\\n   362\\t`{:type :dao.stream.apply/eval-call :segment :pc (pc+1) :env :stack :next\\n   363\\tk}`; `apply2/put-request!` on call-in; on `ok` add the call-out wait entry\\n   364\\tcorrelated by the parked id; on `full` retain the request in a\\n   365\\t`:request-sent` wait entry, as the walker does; the other outcomes fail the\\n   366\\tcall naming the outcome. The host side (`ffi/bridge-step`, `ffi/maybe-run`)\\n   367\\tis used unchanged. `call-result` and `call-response-wait-entry` are private\\n   368\\tin `ast_walker.cljc` today; Phase 0 lifts both into `yin.vm.ffi` so that\\n   369\\tcorrelation checking is written once (this is the one edit to an existing\\n   370\\tfile the roadmap requires).\\n   371\\t\\n   372\\t### 3.5 Scheduler, park, resume\\n   373\\t\\n   374\\tThe VM record carries the engine's scheduler fields (`:ready-queue`,\\n   375\\t`:wait-set`, `:parked`, `:id-counter`, `:store`, `:blocked?`, `:halted?`)\\n   376\\twith the same meanings. Wait entries built by the semantic `park-entry-fns`\\n   377\\tare `{:segment :pc :env :stack :k :reason :cursor-ref/:stream-id}` — pure\\n   378\\tdata: no resolved stream handle and no resume closure is ever attached to a\\n   379\\twait or ready entry, so the wait set of a blocked machine survives an EDN\\n   380\\tround-trip (§1.1's serialization limit; the program's own `env`/stack values\\n   381\\taside). `engine/check-wait-set` resolves handles from the store on every\\n   382\\tpoll, and restoration is dispatched explicitly. The restore function is\\n   383\\t\\n   384\\t```\\n   385\\tsemantic-restore : base entry val → base with control {:segment :pc},\\n   386\\t                                       env, stack, k, val from entry\\n   387\\t```\\n   388\\t\\n   389\\tand is handed to `engine/resume-from-run-queue` and\\n   390\\t`engine/resume-continuation`, exactly as `ast-walker-restore` is;\\n   391\\t`engine/resume-from-run-queue` runs the terminal-outcome check (a woken\\n   392\\tretry that ended `closed`/`cursor-mismatch`/… fails as the immediate\\n   393\\toperation would) before restoring. No `:restore-fn` is passed into\\n   394\\t`engine/handle-effect` or `engine/check-wait-set` from this evaluator, so\\n   395\\tnothing live rides on an entry. `engine/run-loop` drives `run`.\\n   396\\t\\n   397\\t### 3.6 Observation and traces\\n   398\\t\\n   399\\tProgram observation is `stream-observer`, above the VM. For *execution*\\n   400\\tobservation the design reserves a trace vocabulary emitted at park points\\n   401\\tand, when enabled, per instruction:\\n   402\\t\\n   403\\t```\\n   404\\t[cfg :yin.trace/segment seg t] [cfg :yin.trace/pc pc t] [cfg :yin.trace/val v t]\\n   405\\t[cfg :yin.trace/env-ref e t]   [cfg :yin.trace/k-depth d t]\\n   406\\t```\\n   407\\t\\n   408\\twith `t` the step counter. This is the §6 endgame of the cesk-space plan:\\n   409\\tthe machine executes from the image and *emits* its configuration, so\\n   410\\t`as-of` and causal debugging are queries over a stream, not interpretation\\n   411\\tfrom one. `yin.vm.telemetry` is a stub that rejects a non-nil option; the\\n   412\\ttrace lands there when telemetry is designed, and nothing in Phase 1–3\\n   413\\tdepends on it. It is listed so the hot loop is written with the emission\\n   414\\tseam in one place (the `cesk-return` equivalent) rather than retrofitted.\\n   415\\t\\n   416\\t---\\n   417\\t\\n   418\\t## §4. CESK semantics on linear datoms\\n   419\\t\\n   420\\t### 4.1 Configuration\\n   421\\t\\n   422\\t$$\\\\langle C, E, S, K \\\\rangle \\\\quad\\\\text{where}\\\\quad\\n   423\\tC = \\\\langle seg, pc, val, St \\\\rangle$$\\n   424\\t\\n   425\\t- **C (control)**: the segment id, the program counter, the accumulator\\n   426\\t  `val`, and the operand stack `St` (a vector; `St` is control, not store,\\n   427\\t  because it is part of what the machine is *doing*, and it is saved in\\n   428\\t  frames).\\n   429\\t- **E (environment)**: a persistent map `sym → value`, extended on closure\\n   430\\t  entry with `merge closure-env (zipmap params args)`, as the walker does.\\n   431\\t  Lexical addressing is reserved (§6.4).\\n   432\\t- **S (store)**: the map `key → value` holding `yin/def` results, stream\\n   433\\t  handles, cursor entries, and the FFI pair — unchanged from `engine`.\\n   434\\t- **K (continuation)**: a vector of frames, innermost last. Frame kinds:\\n   435\\t  `{:type :return :segment :pc :env :stack-base}` for calls, and the\\n   436\\t  effect-continuation frames the engine already defines\\n   437\\t  (`:dao.stream.apply/eval-call`, `:request-sent`), extended with\\n   438\\t  `:segment :pc :stack`.\\n   439\\t\\n   440\\tLet `I = code[seg][pc]` be the decoded instruction and `I.x` its operands.\\n   441\\t\\n   442\\t### 4.2 Transitions for the core instructions\\n   443\\t\\n   444\\t$$\\\\begin{aligned}\\n   445\\t&\\\\text{const}:&& \\\\langle seg,pc,val,St,E,S,K\\\\rangle \\\\to \\\\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\\\,I.v,\\\\,St,E,S,K\\\\rangle\\\\\\\\\\n   446\\t&\\\\text{var}:&& \\\\to \\\\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\\\,\\\\rho(E,S,I.name),\\\\,St,E,S,K\\\\rangle\\\\\\\\\\n   447\\t&\\\\text{closure}:&& \\\\to \\\\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\\\,\\\\mathrm{clo}(I.params,I.entry,seg,E),\\\\,St,E,S,K\\\\rangle\\\\\\\\\\n   448\\t&\\\\text{push}:&& \\\\to \\\\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\\\,val,\\\\,St \\\\Vert [val],E,S,K\\\\rangle\\\\\\\\\\n   449\\t&\\\\text{jump}:&& \\\\to \\\\langle seg,\\\\,I.target,\\\\,val,St,E,S,K\\\\rangle\\\\\\\\\\n   450\\t&\\\\text{branch-false}:&& \\\\to \\\\langle seg,\\\\,(val\\\\ ?\\\\ pc{+}1 : I.target),\\\\,val,St,E,S,K\\\\rangle\\\\\\\\\\n   451\\t&\\\\text{halt}:&& \\\\to \\\\text{halted},\\\\ \\\\text{result} = val\\n   452\\t\\\\end{aligned}$$\\n   453\\t\\n   454\\twhere $\\\\rho$ is `engine/resolve-var` (env → store → primitives → modules).\\n   455\\t\\n   456\\t**call** with $n = I.argc$, $St = St' \\\\Vert [f, a_1..a_n]$:\\n   457\\t\\n   458\\t$$\\\\begin{aligned}\\n   459\\t&f = \\\\mathrm{clo}(ps, e, seg', E_c),\\\\ \\\\neg tail:&&\\n   460\\t\\\\to \\\\langle seg',\\\\,e,\\\\,val,\\\\,St',\\\\,E_c[ps \\\\mapsto a],\\\\,S,\\\\,K \\\\Vert [\\\\mathrm{ret}(seg,pc{+}1,E,|St'|)]\\\\rangle\\\\\\\\\\n   461\\t&f = \\\\mathrm{clo}(ps, e, seg', E_c),\\\\ tail:&&\\n   462\\t\\\\to \\\\langle seg',\\\\,e,\\\\,val,\\\\,St',\\\\,E_c[ps \\\\mapsto a],\\\\,S,\\\\,K\\\\rangle\\\\\\\\\\n   463\\t&f\\\\ \\\\text{primitive},\\\\ f(a)\\\\ \\\\text{a value } v:&&\\n   464\\t\\\\to \\\\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\\\,v,\\\\,St',E,S,K\\\\rangle\\\\\\\\\\n   465\\t&f\\\\ \\\\text{primitive},\\\\ f(a)\\\\ \\\\text{an effect } \\\\epsilon:&&\\n   466\\t\\\\to \\\\mathrm{effect}(\\\\epsilon, \\\\langle seg,pc{+}1,St',E,S,K\\\\rangle)\\n   467\\t\\\\end{aligned}$$\\n   468\\t\\n   469\\tA tail call grows neither $K$ nor $St$: the walker gets this for free\\n   470\\tbecause a closure body runs against the caller's $k$; the linear machine\\n   471\\tmust state it, and does. Effect handling `effect(ε, cfg)` is\\n   472\\t`engine/handle-effect`; its result is either a value (continue at `pc+1`)\\n   473\\tor a park (the configuration becomes a wait entry, control goes idle).\\n   474\\t\\n   475\\t**return** with $K = K' \\\\Vert [\\\\mathrm{ret}(seg',pc',E',b)]$:\\n   476\\t\\n   477\\t$$\\\\to \\\\langle seg',\\\\,pc',\\\\,val,\\\\,St[0..b),\\\\,E',\\\\,S,\\\\,K'\\\\rangle$$\\n   478\\t\\n   479\\tand with $K = []$: halted with result $val$.\\n   480\\t\\n   481\\t**stream-next** (cursor ref $c = val$), by outcome of `next` on the handle\\n   482\\tbehind $c$:\\n   483\\t\\n   484\\t$$\\\\begin{aligned}\\n   485\\t&ok\\\\ v,\\\\ c':&& \\\\to \\\\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\\\,v,\\\\,St,E,\\\\,S[c \\\\mapsto c'],\\\\,K\\\\rangle\\\\\\\\\\n   486\\t&blocked:&& \\\\to \\\\text{parked}\\\\ \\\\{seg,pc{+}1,E,St,K,\\\\ \\\\text{cursor-ref}\\\\ c\\\\}\\\\ \\\\in\\\\ \\\\text{wait-set}\\\\\\\\\\n   487\\t&end:&& \\\\to \\\\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\\\,\\\\mathrm{nil},\\\\,St,E,S,K\\\\rangle\\\\\\\\\\n   488\\t&gap\\\\ c':&& \\\\to \\\\langle seg,pc{+}1,\\\\,\\\\text{:dao.stream/gap},\\\\,St,E,\\\\,S[c \\\\mapsto c'],\\\\,K\\\\rangle\\n   489\\t\\\\end{aligned}$$\\n   490\\t\\n   491\\t**ffi-call** ($St = St' \\\\Vert [a_1..a_n]$): park frame\\n   492\\t$\\\\{\\\\text{eval-call}, seg, pc{+}1, E, St', K\\\\}$ under a fresh id $p$; append\\n   493\\t$\\\\mathrm{request}(p, I.op, a)$ to call-in; on `ok` the wait set gains a\\n   494\\treader entry on the call-out cursor correlated by $p$; when the correlated\\n   495\\tresponse arrives, $val \\\\leftarrow$ its `ok` value (or its `error` raises)\\n   496\\tand control resumes at $pc{+}1$.\\n   497\\t\\n   498\\t**park**: $K$, $E$, $St$, $seg$, $pc{+}1$ are written to `:parked` under a\\n   499\\tfresh id; the VM halts with that record as its value.\\n   500\\t**resume** ($val$ = resume value, $I.id$ the parked id): the parked\\n   501\\tconfiguration is restored with $val$ as its accumulator.\\n   502\\t**current-continuation**: $val \\\\leftarrow \\\\{seg, pc{+}1, E, St, K\\\\}$\\n   503\\treified as data.\\n   504\\t\\n   505\\t### 4.3 What the equations make visible\\n   506\\t\\n   507\\tCompare the walker's evaluation of one operand: it allocates a frame map,\\n   508\\t`assoc`s the frame into $k$, `conj`s onto `:evaluated`, and dispatches on a\\n   509\\tkeyword `:type` twice (node, then continuation). The linear machine does\\n   510\\t`push` (one vector `conj`) and increments `pc`. The \\\"continuation\\\" of an\\n   511\\toperand sequence is the *next pc*, a fact the lowering already computed.\\n   512\\tThis is the entire performance argument, stated as semantics rather than as\\n   513\\tengineering (§6).\\n   514\\t\\n   515\\t---\\n   516\\t\\n   517\\t## §5. Lowering: Universal AST → linear datoms\\n   518\\t\\n   519\\t### 5.1 Placement and contract\\n   520\\t\\n   521\\t`yin.vm.linearize` (not `yang.linearize`): yang owns syntax → AST and must\\n   522\\tstay ignorant of any evaluator; lowering AST → executable code is VM lineage\\n   523\\tand must stay ignorant of any surface syntax. Clojure, Python and PHP front\\n   524\\tends all reach the linearizer through the one canonical form. The\\n   525\\trecommendation departs from the target-file example in the task brief for\\n   526\\tthis reason.\\n   527\\t\\n   528\\t```\\n   529\\tlower : [:yin/* datoms] → [:yin.code/* datoms]         ; pure, total over supported node types\\n   530\\tlower-ast : ast-map → [:yin.code/* datoms]             ; convenience = lower ∘ ast->datoms\\n   531\\t```\\n   532\\t\\n   533\\tInput is canonical AST datoms because that is what travels and what the\\n   534\\twalker consumes; the implementation may reconstruct maps with\\n   535\\t`vm/datoms->ast` internally in Phase 2 and lower from the datom index\\n   536\\tdirectly later. Output is one segment per program root.\\n   537\\t\\n   538\\t### 5.2 When: ahead-of-time at the load boundary\\n   539\\t\\n   540\\tCompilation is AOT per segment, once, where the program crosses into the\\n   541\\tVM: `eval` lowers its AST; a composition whose program stream carries AST\\n   542\\tdatoms composes `lower` into the loader it hands the observer; a composition\\n   543\\twhose stream carries code datoms hands the loader alone. There is no\\n   544\\ttiering, no recompile-at-boundary, no dirty flag: the engine's versioned\\n   545\\tcompile cache exists for programs that grow by append, and a segment is\\n   546\\timmutable once emitted. Streaming delivery of a growing segment (a pc past\\n   547\\tthe loaded length parks on code rather than erroring) is reserved for a\\n   548\\tlater phase; it is additive because a pc beyond `length` is already a\\n   549\\tdefined load-time condition.\\n   550\\t\\n   551\\t### 5.3 The flattening\\n   552\\t\\n   553\\tRecursive descent over the AST in evaluation order, emitting instructions\\n   554\\tinto a growing vector and lambda bodies out of line:\\n   555\\t\\n   556\\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   557\\t| AST node                                                | Emitted sequence                                                   |\\n   558\\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   559\\t| `:literal v`                                            | `const v`                                                          |\\n   560\\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   561\\t| `:variable s`                                           | `var s`                                                            |\\n   562\\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   563\\t| `:lambda ps body`                                       | `closure ps →L` ; body emitted later at label L ending in `return` |\\n   564\\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   565\\t| `:application op args` (tail? τ)                        | `⟦op⟧ push ⟦a₁⟧ push … ⟦aₙ⟧ push call n τ`                         |\\n   566\\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   567\\t| `:if t c a`                                             | `⟦t⟧ branch-false →A ⟦c⟧ jump →E A: ⟦a⟧ E:`                        |\\n   568\\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   569\\t| `:dao.stream.apply/call op args`                        | `⟦a₁⟧ push … ⟦aₙ⟧ push ffi-call op n`                              |\\n   570\\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   571\\t| `:stream/put target val`                                | `⟦target⟧ push ⟦val⟧ stream-put`                                   |\\n   572\\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   573\\t| `:stream/cursor s`, `:stream/next s`, `:stream/close s` | `⟦s⟧ stream-cursor` etc.                                           |\\n   574\\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   575\\t| `:stream/make b`                                        | `stream-make b`                                                    |\\n   576\\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   577\\t| `:vm/store-get k`, `:vm/store-put k v`, `:vm/gensym p`  | one instruction each                                               |\\n   578\\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   579\\t| `:vm/park`, `:vm/current-continuation`                  | one instruction each                                               |\\n   580\\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   581\\t| `:vm/resume id v`                                       | `⟦v⟧ resume id`                                                    |\\n   582\\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   583\\t| root                                                    | main sequence ends in `halt`; bodies follow                        |\\n   584\\t+---------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n   585\\t\\n   586\\tEvaluation order is the walker's (operator, then operands left to right),\\n   587\\tso effect order is identical, which the parity suite checks. Tail position\\n   588\\tis read from `:yin/tail?` as yang already marks it; `:if` branches inherit\\n   589\\tit; nothing new is inferred. Labels are resolved to instruction refs in a\\n   590\\tsecond pass; the linearizer emits refs, never pc integers.\\n   591\\t\\n   592\\tEvery instruction gets `:yin.code/source` = the AST node eid it came from,\\n   593\\tand the segment gets `:yin.code/derived-from` = the root eid. The reverse\\n   594\\tdirection (render an instruction range back to source) is a query, not a\\n   595\\tfunction in this namespace.\\n   596\\t\\n   597\\t---\\n   598\\t\\n   599\\t## §6. Performance architecture\\n   600\\t\\n   601\\t### 6.1 Traversal mechanics\\n   602\\t\\n   603\\tWalker, per AST node: read `:type` (keyword map lookup), destructure the\\n   604\\tnode (2–3 lookups), allocate a continuation frame, and later read the frame\\n   605\\tback (`:frame`, `:operands`, `:evaluated`, `:env`, `:next`: 5 lookups) and\\n   606\\tdispatch on `:type` of the frame. Nodes are heap-scattered persistent maps\\n   607\\treached by pointer from their parent.\\n   608\\t\\n   609\\tLinear machine, per instruction: `aget` on one array, one integer `case`,\\n   610\\tone operation on locals. Operands are fields of the decoded instruction\\n   611\\t(array slots or record fields), read once. The next instruction is the next\\n   612\\tarray slot.\\n   613\\t\\n   614\\t### 6.2 Memory and allocation\\n   615\\t\\n   616\\t+--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\\n   617\\t| Event                    | Walker allocations                         | Linear allocations                                     |\\n   618\\t+--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\\n   619\\t| evaluate an operand      | frame map, `assoc k`, `conj evaluated` (3) | `conj` onto `St` (1)                                   |\\n   620\\t+--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\\n   621\\t| begin an application     | frame map (1)                              | 0                                                      |\\n   622\\t+--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\\n   623\\t| test an `if`             | frame map (1)                              | 0                                                      |\\n   624\\t+--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\\n   625\\t| call a closure           | `zipmap`, `merge` (2)                      | `zipmap`, `merge`, return frame (3), 2 if tail         |\\n   626\\t+--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\\n   627\\t| every non-hot transition | one `ASTWalkerVM` record                   | 0 (registers stay in `loop` locals until a park point) |\\n   628\\t+--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\\n   629\\t\\n   630\\tFor `(+ x 1)` the walker performs roughly nine allocations and a dozen map\\n   631\\tlookups across six transitions; the linear machine performs three vector\\n   632\\t`conj`s and one argument vector across seven instructions. Call-heavy code\\n   633\\t(the countdown benchmark is nothing but calls and an `if`) is where the\\n   634\\tdifference lands. The remaining allocations are the persistent `St` and the\\n   635\\tenv `merge`; both have reserved optimizations (§6.4) that require no datom\\n   636\\tchange.\\n   637\\t\\n   638\\t### 6.3 Cache locality\\n   639\\t\\n   640\\tThe image is one contiguous array of small decoded records built in pc\\n   641\\torder, so sequential execution walks memory sequentially; a loop body is a\\n   642\\tfew cache lines revisited. Walker nodes are wherever the reader or\\n   643\\t`datoms->ast` allocated them, and each continuation frame is a fresh\\n   644\\tallocation on the nursery. On V8 and Dart, where keyword `case` compiles to\\n   645\\thash-and-compare chains and small maps are dictionary-mode objects, the\\n   646\\tinteger switch and array indexing matter more than on HotSpot; the historic\\n   647\\tbenchmarks put semantic bytecode at 2–7x slower on those hosts precisely\\n   648\\tbecause it did graph traversal and map lookups per step, both of which this\\n   649\\tdesign removes.\\n   650\\t\\n   651\\t### 6.4 Relation to bytecode, and what is reserved\\n   652\\t\\n   653\\tThe loaded image *is* bytecode. The difference from a traditional bytecode\\n   654\\tVM is only the stored form (five datoms per instruction rather than a few\\n   655\\tbytes) and the load step that decodes it. Execution cost is therefore\\n   656\\tbytecode-class by construction, and queryability is not paid for on the hot\\n   657\\tpath at all — it is paid once at load. The historic 1.0–1.1x register/stack\\n   658\\tline in `cesk-space-optimization.md` is the target; the walker sits at\\n   659\\t1.32x on that workload, the old graph-walking semantic VM at 1.69x.\\n   660\\t\\n   661\\tReserved, additive, none required for Phase 1–3:\\n   662\\t\\n   663\\t- **Lexical addressing**: `:var` gains `:yin.code/depth`/`:yin.code/index`\\n   664\\t  when the linearizer proves the name lexically bound; frames become vectors;\\n   665\\t  only unbound names walk store → primitives → modules.\\n   666\\t- **Superinstructions**: `:push-const`, `:push-var` fused by the loader (not\\n   667\\t  the linearizer, so datoms stay minimal).\\n   668\\t- **Host-array operand stack** inside the hot loop, materialized to a vector\\n   669\\t  only at park points (\\\"immutability observable only at park points\\\",\\n   670\\t  streams note §3).\\n   671\\t- **Content-addressed segments** (`:yin.code/hash`) for dedup across VMs and\\n   672\\t  for verifying a shipped continuation against its code.\\n   673\\t\\n   674\\tPhase 4 measured it: see §8.\\n   675\\t\\n   676\\t## §7. The Universal Continuation Format (Proposed)\\n   677\\t\\n   678\\t> [!WARNING]  \\n   679\\t> **Status: Proposed / Deferred.** The Semantic VM's linear CESK state is theoretically sound as an architecture-agnostic continuation format, but true heterogeneous network migration requires addressing several critical defects identified in the 2026-09-14 architectural review.\\n   680\\t\\n   681\\tThe Semantic VM's linear CESK state (`{:segment id, :pc n, :env E, :stack S, :k K}`) is proposed as the canonical exchange format for network-transparent continuations. Because this lowered representation resolves execution-order ambiguity inherent in the Universal AST, it provides a simpler target for specialized execution engines (e.g., WebAssembly, LLVM, hardware FPGA) to participate in the `datom.world` ecosystem.\\n   682\\t\\n   683\\tTo safely \\\"lift on park\\\" and \\\"lower on resume\\\" across heterogeneous boundaries without violating host isolation or concurrency invariants, the following contracts must be fully specified before this feature is accepted:\\n   684\\t\\n   685\\t1. **Safepoint Reconstruction Metadata:** `:yin.code/source` mappings are insufficient for state reconstruction. The compiler must generate exact safepoint metadata tying canonical PC locations to physical register/stack mappings so that arbitrary hardware states can be predictably lifted into the canonical CESK format.\\n   686\\t2. **Recursive Portable Encoding:** Section 1.1 permits host functions and local stream handles in the environment. A recursive serialization protocol is required to encode these local references into portable descriptors, with explicit failure modes for un-serializable resources.\\n   687\\t3. **Dependency Closure & Context:** A parked fragment is not a complete configuration. The format must explicitly declare its required primitives, loaded modules, and the global store state required for valid resumption.\\n   688\\t4. **Ownership Arbitration:** Emitting a continuation does not transfer ownership. The transport composition must define explicit arbitration events to prevent source-wakeup races and duplicate resumes by multiple readers.\\n   689\\t5. **Code Identity (Content Addressing):** Because tempids (`:segment 123`) are local to a single machine's database, continuations must refer to code via immutable, versioned semantic profiles (e.g., content hashing) to guarantee the receiving interpreter executes identical logic.\\n   690\\t\\n   691\\t## §8. Phase 4 Benchmarks\\n   692\\t\\n   693\\tMeasured 2026-09-14 on the development machine (macOS, OpenJDK 21.0.2).\\n   694\\t\\n   695\\t**Workload.** The historic tail-recursive countdown from the deleted\\n   696\\t`yin.vm.bytecode-bench`, unchanged, so the numbers read against the table in\\n   697\\t`docs/cesk-space-optimization.md`. **Timed region.** `vm/run` alone: VM\\n   698\\tconstruction, AST → datoms, lowering, and the program load are outside it,\\n   699\\tand every timed call runs the same loaded VM value. **Harness.**\\n   700\\t`test/bench/yin_vm_bench.cljc` (Criterium `quick-bench`; it checks the\\n   701\\tresult is 0 before timing).\\n   702\\t\\n   703\\t### 8.1 JVM\\n   704\\t\\n   705\\t| Mean time per run | n=500 | n=5000 | Ratio to walker (n=5000) |\\n   706\\t|---|---|---|---|\\n   707\\t| AST Walker (v2) | 8.64 ms | 86.96 ms | 1x |\\n   708\\t| **Semantic (v2)** | **1.04 ms** | **10.02 ms** | **0.115x** |\\n   709\\t\\n   710\\tCriterium's standard deviation was under 3% of the mean in every cell. Both\\n   711\\tevaluators scale linearly over the 10x (walker 10.1x, semantic 9.7x).\\n   712\\t\\n   713\\t**Against the target line.** The historic register and stack VMs are deleted,\\n   714\\tso the like-for-like reading is the ratio to the walker measured in the same\\n   715\\trun. The historic register VM ran at 1/1.32 = 0.76x the walker and stack at\\n   716\\t0.80x; the semantic VM runs at 0.115x, about 6.6x under that line rather than\\n   717\\tat it. Absolute times are not comparable across the two tables: this v2\\n   718\\twalker measures 86.96 ms against the historic v1 walker's 29.33 ms on a\\n   719\\tdifferent run, and the gap reflects the machine and v2's walker itself, not\\n   720\\tthe semantic VM. The target is met with margin.\\n   721\\t\\n   722\\t### 8.2 Node.js and Dart\\n   723\\t\\n   724\\tMeasured on the host machine on 2026-09-14 with:\\n   725\\t\\n   726\\t```sh\\n   727\\tclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile vm-bench\\n   728\\tnode target/vm-bench.js 500 5000\\n   729\\t\\n   730\\tclj -M:cljd compile yin.register-bench-cljd-v2\\n   731\\tdart run bin/register_bench_cljd_v2.dart                   # both evaluators, n=50000\\n   732\\t```\\n   733\\t\\n   734\\t| Mean time per run | Node.js (n=5000) | Dart (n=50000) | Ratio to walker |\\n   735\\t|---|---|---|---|\\n   736\\t| AST Walker (v2) | 71.30 ms | 184 ms | 1x |\\n   737\\t| **Semantic (v2)** | **19.82 ms** | **21 ms** | **0.278x (Node), 0.114x (Dart)** |\\n   738\\t\\n   739\\t§6.3 predicted the gap would widen on these hosts, since keyword `case` and\\n   740\\tmap lookups cost more there and the linear machine removes both from the hot\\n   741\\tpath. Only part of that holds. On Dart the semantic VM runs at 0.114x the\\n   742\\twalker, the same as the JVM's 0.115x rather than a wider gap. On Node the gap\\n   743\\tis narrower: 0.278x, about 3.6x faster than the walker against 8.7x on the\\n   744\\tJVM. Both hosts still clear the historic 0.76x register-VM line.\\n   745\\t\\n   746\\t### 8.3 Default decision\\n   747\\t\\n   748\\tThe semantic VM meets the performance line on the JVM, so `yin.repl.core`\\n   749\\tnow defaults to `:semantic`; `(vm :ast-walker)` still selects the walker.\\n   750\\tBehavioural parity for the switch rests on the Phase 1–3 suites and on a\\n   751\\tREPL corpus that produced identical output under both evaluators. The corpus\\n   752\\tcovered arithmetic, `def`/`defn`, `*1` history, 10⁵ tail calls, 2·10⁴-deep\\n   753\\tnon-tail recursion, streams, Python and PHP input, datom literals, and error\\n   754\\ttext. Where the two still differ is recorded in\\n   755\\t[`yin.vm.divergence-register.md`](./yin.vm.divergence-register.md#the-semantic-vm-against-the-ast-walker).\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'python3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_8\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'python3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"2026-09-14 02:30:50.965 xcodebuild[77937:3233730]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-09-14 02:30:51.136 xcodebuild[77937:3233729] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\npython3: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\npython3: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-FmW9H0pO' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-09-14 02:30:51.652 xcodebuild[77941:3233751]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-09-14 02:30:51.819 xcodebuild[77941:3233750] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\n# Code Review Summary for `working`\\n\\n## Executive Overview\\n- **Total Files Changed:** 6\\n- **Lines Added:** +314 | **Lines Deleted:** -103\\n- **Test-to-Source Additions Ratio:** 0.35 (44 test lines vs 127 source lines)\\n- **Overall Change Risk:** `MEDIUM`\\n\\n### Included Commits\\n- 352979f feat(semantic-vm): Phase 3 Streams, FFI, consumers and handoff demo\\n- 2bf3977 feat(semantic-vm): Phase 1 Core Linear Interpreter & Pure-data wait sets\\n- 2b4e081 feat(semantic-vm): Phase 2 AST Lowering Compiler\\n- 2ce4451 docs: add §7 Universal Continuation Format as proposed feature\\n- 394940e docs: append Universal Continuation Format to semantic vm design\\n\\n## Automated Scan Findings\\n✅ No critical security or code quality smells detected by automated scan.\\n\\n## File Churn & Risk Breakdown\\n| File Path | Category | +Add | -Del | Risk |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| `src/cljc/yin/repl/core.cljc` | Source Code | +79 | -44 | `Medium` |\\n| `src/cljd/yin/register_bench_cljd_v2.cljd` | Source Code | +44 | -36 | `Medium` |\\n| `shadow-cljs.edn` | Source Code | +4 | -0 | `Medium` |\\n| `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md` | Documentation | +68 | -15 | `Low` |\\n| `docs/design/yin.vm.divergence-register.md` | Documentation | +75 | -2 | `Low` |\\n| `test/yin/repl_core_test.cljc` | Tests | +44 | -6 | `Low` |\\n\\n## Reviewer Focus & Recommendations\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_9\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat deps.edn test/bench/yin_vm_bench.cljc src/cljd/yin/register_bench_cljd_v2.cljd; cat src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_9\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat deps.edn test/bench/yin_vm_bench.cljc src/cljd/yin/register_bench_cljd_v2.cljd; cat src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"{:deps {org.clojure/core.async {:mvn/version \\\"1.8.741\\\"}\\n        ring/ring {:mvn/version \\\"1.14.1\\\"}\\n        com.cognitect/transit-clj {:mvn/version \\\"1.0.333\\\"}\\n        com.datomic/peer {:mvn/version \\\"1.0.7364\\\"}\\n        org.postgresql/postgresql {:mvn/version \\\"42.7.3\\\"}\\n        org.clojure/data.json {:mvn/version \\\"2.5.0\\\"}\\n        http-kit/http-kit {:mvn/version \\\"2.8.0\\\"}\\n        org.jsoup/jsoup {:mvn/version \\\"1.18.3\\\"}\\n        instaparse/instaparse {:mvn/version \\\"1.5.0\\\"}\\n        criterium/criterium {:mvn/version \\\"0.4.6\\\"}\\n        stigmergy/chp {:mvn/version \\\"0.1.0-SNAPSHOT\\\"\\n                       :exclusions [refactor-nrepl/refactor-nrepl]}}\\n :cljd/opts {}\\n :paths [\\\"src/clj\\\" \\\"src/cljc\\\" \\\"src/cljs\\\" \\\"src/cljd\\\"]\\n :aliases {:clean {:extra-paths [\\\"src/dev\\\"]\\n                   :exec-fn clean/clean}\\n           :profile-btree {:extra-paths [\\\"src/dev\\\"]\\n                           :extra-deps {persistent-sorted-set/persistent-sorted-set\\n                                        {:mvn/version \\\"0.3.0\\\"}}\\n                           :main-opts [\\\"-m\\\" \\\"btree-bench\\\"]}\\n           :test {:extra-paths [\\\"test\\\"]\\n                  :extra-deps {io.github.cognitect-labs/test-runner\\n                               {:git/tag \\\"v0.5.1\\\" :git/sha \\\"dfb30dd\\\"}}\\n                  :main-opts [\\\"-m\\\" \\\"cognitect.test-runner\\\"]\\n                  :exec-fn cognitect.test-runner.api/test}\\n           :nREPL {:extra-paths [\\\"test\\\"]\\n                   :extra-deps {nrepl/nrepl {:mvn/version \\\"1.0.0\\\"}\\n                                cider/cider-nrepl {:mvn/version \\\"0.58.0\\\"}}\\n                   :main-opts [\\\"-m\\\" \\\"nrepl.cmdline\\\" \\\"--middleware\\\" \\\"[cider.nrepl/cider-middleware]\\\"]}\\n           :cljs {:extra-paths [\\\"test\\\" \\\"src/dev\\\"]\\n                  :extra-deps {thheller/shadow-cljs {:mvn/version \\\"3.3.5\\\"}\\n                               reagent/reagent {:mvn/version \\\"2.0.1\\\"}\\n                               com.cognitect/transit-cljs {:mvn/version \\\"0.8.280\\\"}}}\\n           :dev {:extra-paths [\\\"src/dev\\\"]\\n                 :extra-deps {thheller/shadow-cljs {:mvn/version \\\"3.3.5\\\"}\\n                              cider/cider-nrepl {:mvn/version \\\"0.58.0\\\"}\\n                              reagent/reagent {:mvn/version \\\"2.0.1\\\"}\\n                              com.cognitect/transit-cljs {:mvn/version \\\"0.8.280\\\"}\\n                              ;; benchmark baseline + psset-fixtures\\n                              ;; generator only; production uses\\n                              ;; dao.data.btree\\n                              persistent-sorted-set/persistent-sorted-set\\n                              {:mvn/version \\\"0.3.0\\\"}}\\n                 :jvm-opts [\\\"-Dconfig=config.clj\\\"]}\\n           :cljd {:extra-paths [\\\"test\\\"]\\n                  :extra-deps {tensegritics/clojuredart\\n                               {:git/url \\\"https://github.com/tensegritics/ClojureDart.git\\\"\\n                                :sha \\\"81b5c03a55cf52b21dc0be8ccfa4827b9889f488\\\"}}\\n                  :main-opts [\\\"-m\\\" \\\"cljd.build\\\"]\\n                  :cljd/opts {:kind :flutter :main datomworld.demo.main}}\\n           :server {:extra-deps {http-kit/http-kit {:mvn/version \\\"2.8.0\\\"}}\\n                    :jvm-opts [\\\"-Dconfig=config.clj\\\"]\\n                    :main-opts [\\\"-m\\\" \\\"world.server\\\"]}\\n           :clj-yin-repl {:main-opts [\\\"-m\\\" \\\"yin.repl\\\"]}\\n           :cljs-yin-repl\\n           {:extra-paths [\\\"test\\\" \\\"src/dev\\\"]\\n            :extra-deps {thheller/shadow-cljs {:mvn/version \\\"3.3.5\\\"}\\n                         reagent/reagent {:mvn/version \\\"2.0.1\\\"}\\n                         com.cognitect/transit-cljs {:mvn/version \\\"0.8.280\\\"}}\\n            :main-opts [\\\"-m\\\" \\\"shadow.cljs.devtools.cli\\\" \\\"run\\\" \\\"yin.repl/-main\\\"]}\\n           :clj-yin-repl {:main-opts [\\\"-m\\\" \\\"yin.repl\\\"]}\\n           :cljs-yin-repl\\n           {:extra-paths [\\\"test\\\" \\\"src/dev\\\"]\\n            :extra-deps {thheller/shadow-cljs {:mvn/version \\\"3.3.5\\\"}\\n                         reagent/reagent {:mvn/version \\\"2.0.1\\\"}\\n                         com.cognitect/transit-cljs {:mvn/version \\\"0.8.280\\\"}}\\n            :main-opts [\\\"-m\\\" \\\"shadow.cljs.devtools.cli\\\" \\\"run\\\" \\\"yin.repl/-main\\\"]}\\n           :fmt {:extra-paths [\\\"src/dev\\\"]\\n                 :exec-fn tasks/fix-all}\\n           :kondo {:extra-deps {clj-kondo/clj-kondo {:mvn/version \\\"2026.01.19\\\"}}\\n                   :main-opts [\\\"-m\\\" \\\"clj-kondo.main\\\"]}\\n           :telemetry-server {:main-opts [\\\"-m\\\" \\\"yin.vm.telemetry-server.jvm\\\"]}\\n           :dbg {:extra-paths [\\\"test\\\"]\\n                 :main-opts [\\\"-m\\\" \\\"debug-cljd\\\"]\\n                 :cljd/opts {:kind :flutter :main datomworld.demo.main}}\\n           :cljd-yin-repl\\n           {:extra-deps {tensegritics/clojuredart\\n                         {:git/url \\\"https://github.com/tensegritics/ClojureDart.git\\\"\\n                          :sha \\\"81b5c03a55cf52b21dc0be8ccfa4827b9889f488\\\"}}\\n            :main-opts [\\\"-m\\\" \\\"yin.repl.runner\\\"]}\\n           :cljd-yin-repl-build\\n           {:extra-deps {tensegritics/clojuredart\\n                         {:git/url \\\"https://github.com/tensegritics/ClojureDart.git\\\"\\n                          :sha \\\"81b5c03a55cf52b21dc0be8ccfa4827b9889f488\\\"}}\\n            :main-opts  [\\\"-m\\\" \\\"cljd.build\\\"]\\n            :cljd/opts  {:kind       :dart\\n                         :main       yin.repl\\n                         :output-dir \\\"build/yin-repl\\\"\\n                         :output-to  \\\"build/yin-repl/yin_repl.dart\\\"}}\\n           :cljd-yin-repl\\n           {:extra-deps {tensegritics/clojuredart\\n                         {:git/url \\\"https://github.com/tensegritics/ClojureDart.git\\\"\\n                          :sha \\\"81b5c03a55cf52b21dc0be8ccfa4827b9889f488\\\"}}\\n            :main-opts [\\\"-m\\\" \\\"yin.repl.runner\\\"]}\\n           :cljd-yin-repl-build\\n           {:extra-deps {tensegritics/clojuredart\\n                         {:git/url \\\"https://github.com/tensegritics/ClojureDart.git\\\"\\n                          :sha \\\"81b5c03a55cf52b21dc0be8ccfa4827b9889f488\\\"}}\\n            :main-opts  [\\\"-m\\\" \\\"cljd.build\\\"]\\n            :cljd/opts  {:kind       :dart\\n                         :main       yin.repl\\n                         :output-dir \\\"build/yin-repl\\\"\\n                         :output-to  \\\"build/yin-repl/yin_repl.dart\\\"}}\\n           :dep-graph {:extra-paths [\\\"src/dev\\\"]\\n                       :extra-deps {org.clojure/tools.namespace {:mvn/version \\\"1.5.0\\\"}}\\n                       :main-opts [\\\"-m\\\" \\\"dep-graph\\\"]}\\n           :atzu {:main-opts [\\\"-m\\\" \\\"agent.tzu\\\"]}}}\\n\\n\\n;; clj -X:fmt\\n\\n;; clj -M:nREPL\\n\\n;; clj -M:nREPL:server\\n;; clj -M:test\\n;; clj -M:test -n yin.vm-literal-test\\n;; clj -M:test -n yin.vm-continuation-test\\n;; clj -M:test -n yang.python-test\\n;; clj -X:test :nses '[yin.vm-literal-test yin.vm-test]'\\n;; clj -M:test -v yin.vm-addition-test/test-lambda-with-multiple-operations\\n\\n;; clj -X:clean\\n\\n;; npx shadow-cljs compile test && node target/node-tests.js\\n;; npx shadow-cljs release demo\\n;; clj -M:dev -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch demo\\n;; clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch demo\\n;; clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile demo\\n;; clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release demo\\n\\n\\n;; mise exec -- clj -M:cljd watch \\n;; mise exec -- flutter run \\n\\n;; To start the emulator:                                                                                              ;; emulator -avd Pixel_3_API_34\\n;; flutter emulators --launch Pixel_3_API_34\\n;; emulator -avd Pixel_3_API_34 -no-snapshot-load  \\n;; emulator -list-avds\\n\\n;;  clj -M:dep-graph                    # full report + dep-graph.dot + dep-graph.svg\\n;;  clj -M:dep-graph --filter \\\"yin.*\\\"   # only yin namespaces\\n;;  clj -M:dep-graph --layout circo     # choose graphviz layout engine\\n(ns bench.yin-vm-bench\\n  \\\"Semantic VM Phase 4: the ast-walker against the linear semantic VM on\\n   `yin.vm` (`docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md` §8).\\n\\n   The workload is the historic tail-recursive countdown from the deleted\\n   `yin.vm.bytecode-bench`, so the ratios read against the 1.32x walker and\\n   1.0–1.1x register/stack line in `docs/cesk-space-optimization.md`.\\n\\n   The timed region is `vm/run` alone. VM construction, AST → datoms, the\\n   lowering, and the program load stay outside it. Each timed call runs the\\n   same loaded VM value: both machines are persistent values and the\\n   countdown touches no stream, so no reset is needed between runs.\\n\\n   JVM (Criterium quick-bench):\\n     clj -Sdeps '{:aliases {:bench {:extra-paths [\\\\\\\"test\\\\\\\"]}}}' \\\\\\\\\\n       -M:bench -m bench.yin-vm-bench 500 5000\\n   Node:\\n     clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile vm-bench\\n     node target/vm-bench.js 500 5000\\\"\\n  (:require #?(:cljd [] :clj [criterium.core :as criterium])\\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\\n            [yin.vm.ast-walker :as ast-walker]\\n            [yin.vm.linearize :as linearize]\\n            [yin.vm.semantic :as semantic])\\n  #?(:cljd (:import [\\\"dart:core\\\" DateTime])))\\n\\n\\n(defn tail-countdown-ast\\n  [n]\\n  (let [self-fn {:type :lambda,\\n                 :params ['self 'n],\\n                 :body {:type :if,\\n                        :test {:type :application,\\n                               :operator {:type :variable, :name '<},\\n                               :operands [{:type :variable, :name 'n}\\n                                          {:type :literal, :value 1}]},\\n                        :consequent {:type :literal, :value 0},\\n                        :alternate\\n                        {:type :application,\\n                         :operator {:type :variable, :name 'self},\\n                         :operands [{:type :variable, :name 'self}\\n                                    {:type :application,\\n                                     :operator {:type :variable, :name '-},\\n                                     :operands [{:type :variable, :name 'n}\\n                                                {:type :literal, :value 1}]}],\\n                         :tail? true},\\n                        :tail? true}}]\\n    {:type :application,\\n     :operator self-fn,\\n     :operands [self-fn {:type :literal, :value n}],\\n     :tail? true}))\\n\\n\\n(defn load-walker\\n  [ast]\\n  (ast-walker/vm-load-program (ast-walker/create-vm) (vm/ast->datoms ast)))\\n\\n\\n(defn load-semantic\\n  [ast]\\n  (semantic/vm-load-program (semantic/create-vm) (linearize/lower-ast ast)))\\n\\n\\n(def evaluators\\n  [[\\\"AST Walker\\\" load-walker] [\\\"Semantic\\\" load-semantic]])\\n\\n\\n(defn- check!\\n  [label loaded]\\n  (let [v (vm/value (vm/run loaded))]\\n    (when-not (= 0 v)\\n      (throw (ex-info \\\"Benchmark workload returned the wrong value\\\"\\n                      {:evaluator label, :value v})))))\\n\\n\\n#?(:cljd nil\\n   :clj\\n   (defn- measure-ms\\n     \\\"Criterium quick-bench mean, in milliseconds.\\\"\\n     [loaded]\\n     (let [result (criterium/quick-benchmark (vm/run loaded) {})]\\n       (criterium/report-result result)\\n       (* 1e3 (first (:mean result))))))\\n\\n\\n#?(:cljd nil\\n   :clj nil\\n   :cljs\\n   (defn- measure-ms\\n     \\\"Mean of 9 samples after 4 warmups, each sample 20 runs, in milliseconds\\n      per run — the sampling discipline of `yin.register-bench-cljd-v2`.\\\"\\n     [loaded]\\n     (let [now #(.now js/performance)\\n           sample (fn []\\n                    (let [start (now)]\\n                      (dotimes [_ 20] (vm/run loaded))\\n                      (/ (- (now) start) 20)))]\\n       (dotimes [_ 4] (sample))\\n       (let [samples (vec (repeatedly 9 sample))]\\n         (println \\\"  samples ms/run:\\\" samples)\\n         (/ (reduce + samples) (count samples))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn run-bench\\n  [ns-list]\\n  (doseq [n ns-list]\\n    (let [ast (tail-countdown-ast n)\\n          means (into {}\\n                      (for [[label load] evaluators]\\n                        (let [loaded (load ast)]\\n                          (check! label loaded)\\n                          (println (str \\\"\\\\n\\\" label \\\" n=\\\" n))\\n                          [label (measure-ms loaded)])))\\n          walker (get means \\\"AST Walker\\\")]\\n      (println (str \\\"\\\\nRESULT n=\\\" n))\\n      (doseq [[label _] evaluators]\\n        (let [ms (get means label)]\\n          (println (str \\\"  \\\" label \\\": \\\" ms \\\" ms/run, \\\"\\n                        (/ ms walker) \\\"x walker\\\")))))))\\n\\n\\n#?(:cljd nil\\n   :clj\\n   (defn -main\\n     [& args]\\n     (run-bench (if (seq args) (map #(Long/parseLong %) args) [500 5000]))\\n     (shutdown-agents)))\\n\\n\\n#?(:cljd nil\\n   :clj nil\\n   :cljs\\n   (defn -main\\n     [& args]\\n     (run-bench (if (seq args) (map #(js/parseInt %) args) [500 5000]))))\\n(ns yin.register-bench-cljd-v2\\n  \\\"Benchmark the Yin VM v2 evaluators on Dart.\\n\\n   `yin.register-bench-cljd` drove the v1 register VM. This benchmark drives\\n   `yin.vm.ast-walker` and the linear `yin.vm.semantic` VM\\n   (`docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md` §8) on one workload, and its numbers\\n   are not comparable with the v1 register numbers. The workload and the\\n   sampling discipline are carried over unchanged so the runs answer the\\n   same question about their own engine.\\n\\n   The timed region is `vm/run` alone. VM construction, the lowering, and the\\n   program load stay outside it: the program is loaded once, and each\\n   iteration resets to that loaded program and runs it, which is the v2\\n   idiom for re-running one program (v2's `reset` preserves `:program`).\\n\\n   Compile and run:\\n     clj -M:cljd compile yin.register-bench-cljd-v2\\n     dart run bin/register_bench_cljd_v2.dart\\\"\\n  (:require [dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ringbuffer]\\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\\n            [yin.vm.ast-walker :as ast-walker]\\n            [yin.vm.linearize :as linearize]\\n            [yin.vm.semantic :as semantic])\\n  (:import [\\\"dart:core\\\" DateTime]))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private benchmark-config\\n  {:workload :tail-countdown,\\n   :iterations 20,\\n   :samples 9,\\n   :warmup 4,\\n   :countdown-n 50000})\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private stream-capacity 1024)\\n\\n\\n(defn- now-ms\\n  []\\n  (/ (.-microsecondsSinceEpoch (DateTime/now)) 1000.0))\\n\\n\\n(defn- mean\\n  [xs]\\n  (/ (reduce + 0 xs) (count xs)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- median\\n  [xs]\\n  (let [sorted (vec (sort xs))\\n        n (count sorted)\\n        mid (quot n 2)]\\n    (if (odd? n)\\n      (nth sorted mid)\\n      (/ (+ (nth sorted (dec mid)) (nth sorted mid)) 2.0))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- closure-call-ast\\n  []\\n  {:type :application,\\n   :operator {:type :lambda, :params ['x], :body {:type :variable, :name 'x}},\\n   :operands [{:type :literal, :value 42}]})\\n\\n\\n(defn- tail-countdown-ast\\n  [n]\\n  (let [self-fn {:type :lambda,\\n                 :params ['self 'n],\\n                 :body {:type :if,\\n                        :test {:type :application,\\n                               :operator {:type :variable, :name '<},\\n                               :operands [{:type :variable, :name 'n}\\n                                          {:type :literal, :value 1}]},\\n                        :consequent {:type :literal, :value 0},\\n                        :alternate\\n                        {:type :application,\\n                         :operator {:type :variable, :name 'self},\\n                         :operands [{:type :variable, :name 'self}\\n                                    {:type :application,\\n                                     :operator {:type :variable, :name '-},\\n                                     :operands [{:type :variable, :name 'n}\\n                                                {:type :literal, :value 1}]}],\\n                         :tail? true},\\n                        :tail? true}}]\\n    {:type :application,\\n     :operator self-fn,\\n     :operands [self-fn {:type :literal, :value n}],\\n     :tail? true}))\\n\\n\\n(defn- workload->ast\\n  [{:keys [workload countdown-n]}]\\n  (case workload\\n    :closure-call (closure-call-ast)\\n    :tail-countdown (tail-countdown-ast countdown-n)\\n    (throw (ex-info \\\"Unknown benchmark workload\\\" {:workload workload}))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- make-stream\\n  \\\"The `:make-stream` the composition hands the VM. A nil capacity is the\\n   VM's default, not an unbounded stream.\\\"\\n  [capacity]\\n  (ringbuffer/create!\\n    {:dao.stream/type ringbuffer/transport-type,\\n     ringbuffer/capacity-key (or capacity vm/default-stream-capacity)}))\\n\\n\\n(defn- load-program\\n  \\\"Construct the VM and load the workload. Construction is all-or-nothing in\\n   v2, so a non-`ok` stream outcome throws here rather than half-building.\\n   The semantic VM refuses an AST in `eval`, so its program is lowered and\\n   loaded explicitly, and it is then run once like the walker's.\\\"\\n  [evaluator ast]\\n  (let [opts {:primitives vm/primitives, :make-stream make-stream}]\\n    (case evaluator\\n      :ast-walker (vm/eval (ast-walker/create-vm opts) ast)\\n      :semantic (vm/run (semantic/vm-load-program\\n                          (semantic/create-vm opts)\\n                          (linearize/lower-ast ast))))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- run-iterations\\n  \\\"Run the already-loaded program `iterations` times. `reset` restores\\n   `:control` from the loaded `:program` and clears the CESK state; `run`\\n   executes it.\\\"\\n  [program iterations]\\n  (dotimes [_ iterations] (vm/run (vm/reset program))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- sample-total-ms\\n  [program iterations]\\n  (let [start (now-ms)]\\n    (run-iterations program iterations)\\n    (- (now-ms) start)))\\n\\n\\n(defn main\\n  []\\n  (doseq [evaluator [:ast-walker :semantic]]\\n    (let [{:keys [workload iterations samples warmup], :as cfg} benchmark-config\\n          ast (workload->ast cfg)\\n          program (load-program evaluator ast)]\\n      (dotimes [_ warmup] (run-iterations program iterations))\\n      (let [sample-total (vec (for [_ (range samples)]\\n                                (sample-total-ms program iterations)))\\n            sample-per-iter (mapv #(/ % iterations) sample-total)]\\n        (println (str \\\"workload=\\\" workload\\n                      \\\" evaluator=\\\" (name evaluator) \\\"-v2\\\"\\n                      \\\" iterations=\\\" iterations\\n                      \\\" samples=\\\" samples\\n                      \\\" warmup=\\\" warmup))\\n        (when (= workload :tail-countdown)\\n          (println (str \\\"countdown_n=\\\" (:countdown-n cfg))))\\n        (println (str \\\"stream_capacity=\\\" stream-capacity))\\n        (println (str \\\"sample_total_ms=\\\" sample-total))\\n        (println (str \\\"sample_per_iter_ms=\\\" sample-per-iter))\\n        (println (str \\\"RESULT mean_total_ms=\\\" (mean sample-total)\\n                      \\\" median_total_ms=\\\" (median sample-total)\\n                      \\\" mean_per_iter_ms=\\\" (mean sample-per-iter)\\n                      \\\" median_per_iter_ms=\\\" (median sample-per-iter)))))))\\n(ns dao.stream.observer\\n  \\\"Observer of one DaoStream medium: attachment, cursor, gap accounting, and\\n   the coordination loop that feeds a consumer one batch at a time.\\n\\n   The medium exists independently of any consumer. Host composition supplies\\n   a unary attach capability and a portable DaoStream descriptor; `attach`\\n   calls that capability exactly once, validates the returned handle against\\n   the reader surface, and mints the observer's cursor at `:dao.stream/oldest`\\n   directly through `dao.stream` — or keeps a cursor handed to it, which is\\n   how a session resumes from where it stopped. The observer never creates,\\n   appends to, or closes the medium, and its complete initial state is\\n   `{:stream handle :cursor cursor :ingress-gaps 0}`.\\n\\n   This namespace requires only `dao.stream` and `dao.stream.observe`,\\n   whose single `step` it loops over. It cannot depend on a ring buffer, a\\n   WebSocket adapter, a resolver representation, a transport key or state, or\\n   any consumer's namespace: transport-specific composition constructs the\\n   unary attacher outside this boundary, for example by partially applying a\\n   dispatch table or a transport resolver. The consumer is opaque here — a\\n   `yin.vm` evaluator, a macro expander, a `dao.space` indexer, or anything\\n   else that can be asked whether it is ready, handed a batch, and run.\\n\\n   A `gap` is recoverable here and only here in the generic machinery. A batch\\n   the observer never saw is advanced past at the recovery cursor and counted,\\n   and observation continues with the next retained batch; silently pretending\\n   the medium was contiguous hides missing data. What a host does with a gap\\n   count is host policy — the REPL shell, for one, reports the loss and\\n   refuses further evaluation until reset — and that policy lives above this\\n   boundary. Terminal read outcomes are errors.\\n\\n   Exports exactly `attach`, `observe-next`, and `run-on-stream`.\\\"\\n  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\\n            [dao.stream.observe :as observe]))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Attachment\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn- attached-handle\\n  \\\"Call the unary capability once and validate its answer, yielding the\\n   handle both `attach` arities build on: a non-`ok` outcome and a handle\\n   without the reader surface throw exactly as `attach` documents.\\\"\\n  [attach! descriptor]\\n  (let [result (attach! descriptor)\\n        outcome (:dao.stream/outcome result)]\\n    (if-not (= :dao.stream/ok outcome)\\n      (throw (ex-info \\\"Stream attachment failed\\\"\\n                      {:dao.stream/outcome outcome,\\n                       :descriptor descriptor,\\n                       :result result}))\\n      (let [handle (:dao.stream/handle result)]\\n        (if-not (stream/reader? handle)\\n          (throw (ex-info \\\"Attached handle declares no reader surface\\\"\\n                          {:descriptor descriptor,\\n                           :surfaces (stream/declared-surfaces handle)}))\\n          handle)))))\\n\\n\\n(defn attach\\n  \\\"Attach to an existing medium and return the initial observer state, or\\n   throw.\\n\\n   `(attach attach! descriptor)` mints the cursor at `:dao.stream/oldest`\\n   and returns `{:stream handle :cursor cursor :ingress-gaps 0}`.\\n   `(attach attach! descriptor {:cursor c :ingress-gaps g})` re-attaches at\\n   a kept cursor, returning `{:stream handle :cursor c :ingress-gaps g}`\\n   without minting one: the composition resuming a session knows where its\\n   previous one stopped, a cursor already covers repositioning, the\\n   transport validates the kept cursor on the first `next`, and the gap\\n   count is seeded from the checkpoint rather than reset to zero.\\n\\n   `attach!` is a unary attachment capability: `(attach! descriptor)`. It is\\n   called exactly once and there is no fallback to stream creation. A\\n   non-`ok` attachment or cursor outcome throws `ex-info` preserving the\\n   original `:dao.stream/outcome`. A handle without the reader surface is a\\n   host assembly error reported with the descriptor and the handle's declared\\n   surfaces, without inventing a DaoStream outcome. Attachment cleanup stays\\n   with the host: this function closes nothing, and a failure leaves no\\n   partial observer behind.\\\"\\n  ([attach! descriptor]\\n   (let [handle (attached-handle attach! descriptor)\\n         minted (stream/cursor handle stream/anchor-oldest)\\n         mint-outcome (:dao.stream/outcome minted)]\\n     (if-not (= :dao.stream/ok mint-outcome)\\n       (throw (ex-info \\\"Observer cursor could not be minted\\\"\\n                       {:dao.stream/outcome mint-outcome,\\n                        :descriptor descriptor,\\n                        :result minted}))\\n       {:stream handle,\\n        :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor minted),\\n        :ingress-gaps 0})))\\n  ([attach! descriptor {:keys [cursor ingress-gaps]}]\\n   {:stream (attached-handle attach! descriptor),\\n    :cursor cursor,\\n    :ingress-gaps ingress-gaps}))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Observation\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn- terminal\\n  \\\"Throw, preserving the original read outcome and the observer's own state.\\\"\\n  [message outcome observer]\\n  (throw (ex-info message\\n                  {:dao.stream/outcome outcome,\\n                   :cursor (:cursor observer),\\n                   :ingress-gaps (:ingress-gaps observer)})))\\n\\n\\n(def ^:private uncontinuable-read-outcomes\\n  \\\"The contract's read outcomes this observer cannot continue from.\\\"\\n  #{:dao.stream/cursor-mismatch\\n    :dao.stream/invalid-cursor\\n    :dao.stream/transport-error})\\n\\n\\n(defn- answered-outcome\\n  \\\"The outcome the transport actually answered with.\\n\\n   `observe/step` classifies an answer outside the contract as\\n   `transport-error` and retains the raw answer, so the observer reports what\\n   it was told rather than the classification.\\\"\\n  [read]\\n  (if (contains? read :dao.stream/answer)\\n    (:dao.stream/outcome (:dao.stream/answer read))\\n    (:dao.stream/outcome read)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- observation-terminal\\n  \\\"This observer's policy for a step defect: throw, naming the outcome the\\n   transport answered with.\\\"\\n  [observed observer]\\n  (let [outcome (answered-outcome (:read observed))]\\n    (if (contains? uncontinuable-read-outcomes outcome)\\n      (terminal \\\"Observation cannot continue from this cursor\\\"\\n                outcome\\n                observer)\\n      (terminal \\\"Unexpected DaoStream outcome while observing the medium\\\"\\n                outcome\\n                observer))))\\n\\n\\n(defn- adopt-gap\\n  \\\"This observer's gap policy: commit the recovery cursor and count the loss.\\\"\\n  [observer observed]\\n  (-> observer\\n      (assoc :cursor (:recovery observed))\\n      (update :ingress-gaps (fnil inc 0))))\\n\\n\\n(defn observe-next\\n  \\\"Observe one batch from the attached medium.\\n\\n   Returns `{:status :ok :batch batch :observer observer'}` on `ok`, where the\\n   successor state carries the exact returned cursor; `{:status :blocked\\n   :observer observer}` and `{:status :end :observer observer}`, which retain\\n   the cursor; or `{:status :gap :observer observer'}`, where the successor\\n   carries the recovery cursor and one more counted `:ingress-gaps`. Terminal\\n   outcomes (`cursor-mismatch`, `invalid-cursor`, `transport-error`) and\\n   unexpected ones throw with the original outcome preserved.\\\"\\n  [observer]\\n  (let [observed (observe/step (:stream observer)\\n                               (:cursor observer)\\n                               (fn [_value] {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}))]\\n    (case (:status observed)\\n      :advance {:status :ok,\\n                :batch (:dao.stream/value (:read observed)),\\n                :observer (assoc observer :cursor (:cursor observed))}\\n\\n      :retry {:status :blocked, :observer observer}\\n      :ended {:status :end, :observer observer}\\n      :gap {:status :gap, :observer (adopt-gap observer observed)}\\n\\n      (observation-terminal observed observer))))\\n\\n\\n;; =============================================================================\\n;; Coordination\\n;; =============================================================================\\n\\n(defn- consumer-left-by-run\\n  \\\"The consumer state a failing `run` left, for the carried session.\\n\\n   A `run` that made partial progress before throwing — a flush that\\n   delivered one medium and failed on another — reports the state it reached\\n   by carrying a `:consumer` in its exception's data, so a retry resumes\\n   from that progress rather than repeating delivered work. A `run` that\\n   reports nothing left the state it was handed, which is the furthest\\n   progress the coordination can name for it.\\\"\\n  [e handed]\\n  (let [data (ex-data e)]\\n    (if (contains? data :consumer)\\n      (:consumer data)\\n      handed)))\\n\\n\\n(defn- carry-session\\n  \\\"Rethrow `e`, keeping the throw and carrying the partial session in the\\n   exception's data.\\n\\n   The original message and data are preserved and `:session` is added. The\\n   rethrow is a new `ExceptionInfo` whose cause is the original, so a caller\\n   matching the original exception's concrete type no longer matches the\\n   wrapper — `(ex-cause caught)` names it. Recovery is opt-in: a caller that\\n   does not read `:session` keeps the old semantics — a throw means the\\n   round failed — and a caller that catches resumes from the carried\\n   session, which names the furthest progress the round had made where the\\n   failure happened.\\\"\\n  [session e]\\n  (throw (ex-info (or (ex-message e) (str e))\\n                  (assoc (ex-data e) :session session)\\n                  e)))\\n\\n\\n(defn run-on-stream\\n  \\\"Coordinate one `{:observer observer :consumer consumer}` session over the\\n   attached medium, returning the updated session.\\n\\n   The consumer is any value the three supplied functions agree on. `ready?`\\n   decides when it may accept another batch, `load` hands it one observed\\n   batch and returns the successor consumer, and `run` lets it do whatever\\n   work a loaded batch implies — evaluate, expand, index, forward — and\\n   returns the successor. This is coordination, not interpretation: no field\\n   of the consumer is inspected, so any consumer shape can be driven.\\n\\n   A consumer that is not ready runs first, and observation follows only if\\n   it becomes ready. A ready consumer observes one batch; `gap` commits the\\n   recovery cursor and continues; `blocked` and `end` return the session.\\n   After a batch loads and runs, a consumer that is ready again observes the\\n   next batch, and one that is not returns the session without another read.\\n\\n   A `load` or `run` that throws still propagates — the throw is kept — but\\n   carries the partial session in the exception's data under `:session`, so\\n   recovery is opt-in. Where the failure happened decides the carried\\n   session: a throwing `load` carries the cursor *before* the failing batch,\\n   so a retry re-reads it; a throwing `run` carries the cursor *after* the\\n   loaded batch and the consumer the failing `run` left — the partial state\\n   it reported under `:consumer` in its own exception's data when it made\\n   progress before throwing, else the state `load` returned — so a retry\\n   neither re-reads the batch nor repeats work already delivered; a\\n   terminal read after processed batches carries the session those batches\\n   produced. A consumer whose input can be bad should therefore return that\\n   as data from `load` rather than throw, and reserve the throw for its own\\n   defects.\\\"\\n  [session ready? load run]\\n  (loop [{:keys [observer consumer]} session]\\n    (if-not (ready? consumer)\\n      (let [consumer' (try (run consumer)\\n                           (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\\n                             (carry-session\\n                               {:observer observer,\\n                                :consumer (consumer-left-by-run e consumer)}\\n                               e)))]\\n        (if (ready? consumer')\\n          (recur {:observer observer, :consumer consumer'})\\n          {:observer observer, :consumer consumer'}))\\n      ;; The load is the step's effect, so the cursor advances only once it has\\n      ;; returned: a throwing load propagates before any successor exists, and\\n      ;; the session it carries names the cursor before the failing batch.\\n      (let [observed (try (observe/step (:stream observer)\\n                                        (:cursor observer)\\n                                        (fn [batch]\\n                                          {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok,\\n                                           :dao.stream.observer/loaded\\n                                           (load consumer batch)}))\\n                          (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\\n                            (carry-session {:observer observer, :consumer consumer} e)))]\\n        (case (:status observed)\\n          :advance (let [loaded (:dao.stream.observer/loaded (:effect observed))\\n                         ;; The batch was observed and loaded before the run, so\\n                         ;; a throwing run carries the post-batch cursor and the\\n                         ;; consumer it left: the partial state it reported in\\n                         ;; its exception's data, else the loaded value.\\n                         observer' (assoc observer :cursor (:cursor observed))\\n                         consumer' (try (run loaded)\\n                                        (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\\n                                          (carry-session\\n                                            {:observer observer',\\n                                             :consumer (consumer-left-by-run e loaded)}\\n                                            e)))]\\n                     (if (ready? consumer')\\n                       (recur {:observer observer', :consumer consumer'})\\n                       {:observer observer', :consumer consumer'}))\\n          :gap (recur {:observer (adopt-gap observer observed),\\n                       :consumer consumer})\\n          ;; :retry (blocked) and :ended retain the cursor and end the round.\\n          (:retry :ended) {:observer observer, :consumer consumer}\\n          (try (observation-terminal observed observer)\\n               (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) e\\n                 (carry-session {:observer observer, :consumer consumer} e))))))))\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_10\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '12,115p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md; rg -n 'restore-initial-env|defn.*eval|defn.*reset|session' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc; tail -90 collab/20260914-0226-phase4-fixes.claude-opus-5.stdout.log; tail -60 collab/20260914-0229-phase4-final.claude-opus-5.stdout.log\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_10\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '12,115p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md; rg -n 'restore-initial-env|defn.*eval|defn.*reset|session' src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc; tail -90 collab/20260914-0226-phase4-fixes.claude-opus-5.stdout.log; tail -60 collab/20260914-0229-phase4-final.claude-opus-5.stdout.log\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"## §1. Foundational axioms and invariant compliance\\n\\n### 1.1 The four axioms\\n\\n**Everything is a Stream.** Code arrives on a program stream as a segment\\nvalue; execution is consumption of that stream by an observer, and then\\nconsumption of the segment by the machine. Effects the program performs\\n(`:stream-put`, `:stream-next`, FFI) are emissions on streams the composition\\nsupplied, exactly as for the walker. Optional execution traces are emissions\\ntoo (§3.6). What is *not* a stream is the instruction fetch inside a loaded\\nsegment, and this is deliberate: a boundary belongs \\\"wherever you need\\ndecoupling — and nowhere else\\\" (`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §1). Fetch has\\none consumer, one order, no replay need, and no host boundary; putting a\\nstream there is the per-value tax §4 of that note names as hopeless.\\n\\n**Interpretation Creates Semantics.** `[e :yin.code/op :call]` means nothing\\nuntil `yin.vm.semantic` reads it. The same datoms can be read by a query\\n(\\\"all call sites of this closure\\\"), a renderer (back to source through\\n`:yin.code/source`), a verifier (every block ends in a terminator), or a\\ndifferent VM. The mapping from the keyword mnemonic in the datom to the\\ninteger opcode in the image is the loader's interpretation, not a fact in\\nthe datom. `:yin.code/tail?` is a property of a call in the datoms; only the\\nimage decides it is a distinct `tailcall` dispatch.\\n\\n**Code and State are Datoms.** Instructions are entities with attributes.\\nExecution state is plain data: the control is `{:segment id :pc n}`, the\\nenvironment a map, the operand stack a vector, the continuation a vector of\\nframes each `{:pc :env :stack-base :segment}`. The optional trace vocabulary\\n(§3.6) deposits exactly these as `[e a v t m]` with `t` the step counter,\\nthe \\\"endgame\\\" of `cesk-space-optimization.md` §6: execute fast, emit the\\nmachine image as datoms rather than interpret from them.\\n\\n**Everything is a Continuation.** Park writes `{segment pc env stack k}` into\\n`:parked`; resume reads it back. Nothing in that map is a host object except\\nvalues the program itself put in `env` or on the stack (host functions\\nresolved from primitives, stream handles from the store), which is the same\\nserialization limit the walker has today. A continuation shipped through a\\nstream names its segment by id; the receiving host resolves the id against\\nsegment datoms it holds or was sent alongside.\\n\\n### 1.2 The six invariants\\n\\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Invariant                | How the design honors it                                                         |\\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| No hidden global state   | Opcode table is a value in `yin.vm`. Segments live in the VM record under     |\\n|                          | `:code`. Primitives, modules, `:make-stream`, bridge handlers are all supplied   |\\n|                          | at construction, as for the walker. No `defonce`, no registry.                   |\\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| No implicit control flow | Every transfer of control is an instruction datom: `:jump`, `:branch-false`,     |\\n|                          | `:call`, `:return`, `:halt`. Sequencing is the explicit `:yin.code/pc` order. No |\\n|                          | fallthrough that is not a `pc + 1` visible in the datoms.                        |\\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| No callbacks             | Host effects go through `engine/handle-effect` and the FFI request/response      |\\n|                          | pair; a blocked read parks in the polling wait set. The VM invokes nothing and   |\\n|                          | is invoked by nothing.                                                           |\\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| No shared mutable state  | The image array is built once per segment and never written after load. The hot  |\\n|                          | loop keeps registers in `loop` locals; the persistent VM record is the only      |\\n|                          | state that escapes a step. No mutable deftype fields (a cljd trap on record).    |\\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| No layer collapsing      | Lowering (AST datoms → code datoms), loading (code datoms → image), and          |\\n|                          | execution (image → transitions) are three functions in two namespaces with data  |\\n|                          | between each. The walker keeps interpreting the AST; nothing in the walker       |\\n|                          | changes.                                                                         |\\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| No assumed graphs        | Branch targets and closure bodies are refs; the loader constructs the pc index   |\\n|                          | from tuples in an explicit pass and resolves refs against it. A dangling ref is  |\\n|                          | a load error naming the entity, never a runtime lookup.                          |\\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n\\n---\\n\\n## §2. The linear executable datom specification\\n\\n### 2.1 Tuple format\\n\\nExecutable datoms are canonical 5-tuples `[e a v t m]`, produced the same\\nway `yin.vm/ast->datoms` produces AST datoms: negative tempids for `e`,\\n`t` = 0 unless the linearizer is given one, `m` = `dao.datom/default-op`.\\nAttributes live under the `:yin.code/*` namespace so that\\n`engine/executable-program-datom?` (which selects `:yin/*`) does not confuse\\na code segment with an AST program, and a query can select either view.\\n\\n### 2.2 The segment\\n\\nA segment is one entity plus its instructions. It is the unit a program\\nstream carries (one batch value), the unit the loader accepts, and the unit\\na closure or continuation names.\\n\\n+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Attribute                | Value              | Meaning                                                                          |\\n+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:yin.code/type`         | `:segment`         | Marks the segment entity.                                                        |\\n+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:yin.code/length`       | int                | Number of instructions; pcs are `0 .. length-1`, dense.                          |\\n+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:yin.code/derived-from` | ref (AST root eid) | Provenance; optional when hand-assembled.                                        |\\n+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `:yin.code/hash`         | string             | Reserved: content address of the canonical instruction datoms, for dedup and     |\\n|                          |                    | verification (`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.3). Not required in Phase 1.      |\\n+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n\\nEntry is pc 0 by rule. Making entry a fact would let a segment disagree with\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:645:(defn- vm-reset\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:657:(defn- vm-eval\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:668:    (engine/restore-initial-env initial-env res)))\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:92:(defn restore-initial-env\\nI fixed four of the five defects, and the new REPL tests pass on the JVM (21 tests, 0 failures). The benchmark defect (#4) is still open: both harnesses build, but running `node` and `dart` needs approval, which this session couldn't give.\\n\\n**1. Errors no longer replay (P1).** After a failed evaluation, the shell now reads past the batch that failed and keeps the VM it had before that input. It also prints any output from the failed input once. So after `(do (println \\\"before\\\") (/ 1 0))`, the next `(+ 1 2)` returns `3`, and `*1` keeps working. This also covers a datom batch that fails to load and one that loads but doesn't finish. The fix is in `consume-failed-round` and a `try` around the round in `eval-datoms` (`src/cljc/yin/repl/core.cljc`).\\n\\n**2. `let` scope no longer leaks (P1).** When a program finishes, the VM's environment goes back to what it was before that input, the same way `vm/eval` does it. `(def x 1)`, then `(let [x 7] x)`, then `x` now gives `1`.\\n\\n**3. Reset clears history (P2).** `(reset)` and `(vm …)` now share one helper, `rebuild-session`, which also clears `*1`, `*2` and `*3`. `(*1 4)` after a reset returns an error message instead of crashing.\\n\\n**4. Benchmarks (P2), still open.** I rewrote §8.2 of `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md` to say plainly that both harnesses compile but were never run, and that it has no Node or Dart numbers yet. It gives the commands to run:\\n```sh\\nnode target/vm-bench.js 500 5000\\ndart run bin/register_bench_cljd_v2.dart\\n```\\nIf you run them, or allow `node` and `dart` here, I can add the results to §8.2.\\n\\n**5. Duplicate §7 (P2).** I removed the unconditional Universal Continuation Format section and kept the \\\"Proposed / Deferred\\\" one.\\n\\n**Tests added** to `test/yin/repl_core_test.cljc`:\\n- `a-failed-input-is-consumed-exactly-once`, run under both VMs, plus a case where the batch fails to load.\\n- `lexical-scope-does-not-escape-a-top-level-evaluation`, run under both VMs.\\n- `reset-and-vm-selection-clear-the-value-history`.\\n\\nI didn't run the ClojureScript tests, since they also need `node`. I also didn't check that the new tests fail without the fixes. They reproduce the reviewer's exact scenarios, but they've only been run with the fixes in place. Nothing is committed.\\nI updated the §8.2 benchmark table, but I couldn't commit Phase 4. This session isn't allowed to run `git add` or `git commit`, even as separate commands.\\n\\n**Doc change (`docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md` §8.2):** The \\\"Status: open / no numbers\\\" note is gone. The section now lists the commands and a table with your results:\\n\\n| Mean time per run | Node.js (n=5000) | Dart (n=50000) | Ratio to walker |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| AST Walker (v2) | 71.30 ms | 184 ms | 1x |\\n| **Semantic (v2)** | **19.82 ms** | **21 ms** | **0.278x (Node), 0.114x (Dart)** |\\n\\nI didn't run either benchmark myself; the numbers are the ones from the prompt. I took Dart's n=50000 from the harness default, since the prompt didn't give one.\\n\\n**The results don't back up §6.3's prediction.** §6.3 said the speed gap over the walker would be bigger on V8 and Dart than on the JVM. It isn't:\\n- **Dart:** 0.114x, about the same as the JVM's 0.115x.\\n- **Node:** 0.278x, a smaller gap. The semantic VM is about 3.6x faster than the walker there, against about 8.7x on the JVM.\\n\\nThe section now says this directly. Both hosts still beat the old 0.76x register-VM target. I left the §6.3 prediction (line 644) as written.\\n\\n**To commit:** all six modified tracked files belong to Phase 4: the two design docs, `shadow-cljs.edn`, the REPL core and its test, and the cljd bench. The `collab/` files are untracked logs and I'd leave them out. To commit by hand:\\n\\n```sh\\ngit add docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md docs/design/yin.vm.divergence-register.md \\\\\\n  shadow-cljs.edn src/cljc/yin/repl/core.cljc \\\\\\n  src/cljd/yin/register_bench_cljd_v2.cljd test/yin/repl_core_test.cljc\\ngit commit -m \\\"Finish semantic VM Phase 4: default REPL to :semantic, record divergences and host benchmarks\\\"\\n```\\n\\nOr allow `git add`/`git commit` for this session and I'll run it. The message follows your commit style: an imperative verb, no `feat:` prefix and no Co-Authored-By trailer.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"error\",\"message\":\"You've hit your usage limit. Upgrade to Pro (https://chatgpt.com/explore/pro), visit https://chatgpt.com/codex/settings/usage to purchase more credits or try again at 5:24 AM.\"}\n{\"type\":\"turn.failed\",\"error\":{\"message\":\"You've hit your usage limit. Upgrade to Pro (https://chatgpt.com/explore/pro), visit https://chatgpt.com/codex/settings/usage to purchase more credits or try again at 5:24 AM.\"}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
